explore	V;PST;V.PTCP	explored	Sociologists have explored the adverse consequences of discrimination [3 – 5];
examine	V;PST;V.PTCP	examined	psychologists have examined the mental processes that underpin conscious and unconscious biases [6];
examine	V;PST;V.PTCP	examined	neuroscientists have examined the neurobiological underpinnings of discrimination [7 – 9];
explore	V;PST;V.PTCP	explored	and evolutionary theorists have explored the various ways that in-group / out-group biases emerged across the history of our species. [10]
possess	V;PRS;3;PL	possess	In many respects, researchers already possess a wealth of knowledge concerning the origins and consequences of discrimination and bias. [11]
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	What also should not be lost in discussion of discrimination is the growing push to implement social policy aimed at reducing the occurrence of discriminatory practices.
intend	V;PST;V.PTCP	intended	Mandatory diversity trainings in professional settings, for example, are intended to reduce bias in the workplace by increasing the awareness of employees regarding the challenges facing minority group members. [12]
root	V;PST;V.PTCP	rooted	Indeed, the implementation of certain policies is rooted in the assumption that discrimination and biases are, at least to some appreciable amount, present in modern society.
perceive	V;PST;V.PTCP	perceived	Even so, estimates of the prevalence of perceived discrimination remains rare (see [13 – 14]).
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	At least one prior study by Kessler and colleagues [15], however, using measures of perceived discrimination in a large American sample, reported that approximately 33% of respondents reported some form of discrimination (see also, Gibbons et al. [4]).
seek	V;PRS;SG;3	seeks	The current study seeks to build on this research by estimating the prevalence of discrimination experiences among a large, nationally representative sample of adults from the United States.
address	V;PRS;3;PL	address	Additionally, the analysis address the perceived reasons for reported discrimination experiences.
indicate	V;PST;3;PL	indicated	All respondents who indicated they were discriminated against — specifically, those who responded with sometimes or often to the perceived discrimination measure described above — were asked the following question:
think	V;NFIN	think	“What do you think was the main reason for these experiences?”
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	Respondents were allowed to choose one response from 11 categories.
recode	V;PST;V.PTCP	recoded	For the present analysis, these responses were recoded into nine mutually exclusive categories capturing the following options:
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	The following categories from the original questionnaire were collapsed into one category for the analysis: race; ancestry or national origin; and shade of skin color.
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	Additionally, because this question was only asked of respondents who reported prior discrimination experiences, the built-in skip pattern resulted in a large number of cases scored as missing (legitimate skip).
use	V;PRS;1;PL	use	Because a race variable is not available from the Wave 4 interviews, we use the racial category reported by the respondent during the Wave 1 interview.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Wave 1 race — rather than, say, Wave 3 race — was used to preserve case counts.
appear	V;PST;3;PL	appeared	The logic is that (nearly) all Wave 4 respondents appeared in the Wave 1 sample, but not all would have been interviewed at Wave 3 due to differential patterns of temporary attrition.
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	Respondents were asked to indicate their race from among the following categories: White; Black or African American; Hispanic; American Indian or Native American; and Asian or Pacific Islander.
provide	V;PST;V.PTCP	provided	Respondents were provided the opportunity to select more than one race, and those who did were asked a follow-up question regarding which category best described their racial background.
indicate	V;PST;3;PL	indicated	In the current study, those respondents who indicated more than one race were coded as “mixed race”.
provide	V;NFIN	provide	To provide information on the analytical sample as a whole, two additional demographic variables are included.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	First, age is a continuous measure created by subtracting the year of the respondents' birth (obtained from Wave 1) from the year of the interview at Wave 4.
code	V;PST;V.PTCP	coded	Second, sex was dichotomously coded based on the self-reported sex of the respondent at Wave 4 (0 = female and 1 = male).
include	V;PST;SG;3	included	Our exploratory study included three basic steps.
produce	V;PST;V.PTCP	produced	First, summary statistics of the study variables and racial categories were produced.
examine	V;PST;1;PL	examined	Second, we examined the relative proportions of the two discrimination experience measures across each racial category.
assess	V;PST;1;PL	assessed	Finally, we assessed the distribution of reported reasons for discrimination across the racial categories.
examine	V;NFIN	examine	In order to examine potential bivariate associations, the adjusted F statistic (design-based F) was employed as it corrects for a complex sample design such as that used in the Add Health. [21]
analyze	V;PRS;V.PTCP	analyzing	More specifically, when analyzing weighted sample data employing the svy suite of commands in Stata the conventional Pearson χ2 statistic test of independence is converted into “an Fstatistic with noninteger degrees of freedom by using a second-order Rao and Scott (1981 [22], 1984 [23]) correction”. [24]
associate	V;PST;V.PTCP	associated	The p-value associated with the design-based F is thus more accurate (than the associated with the χ2 statistic) given the adjustments and calculations take into account the weighted nature of the data.
include	V;PST;SG;3	included	This final step also included an examination of the relative distribution of racial categories across the various reported reasons for discrimination.
note	V;PST;V.PTCP	noted	As noted earlier, all analyses were weighted according to the survey weight provided by the Add Health research staff and standard errors were corrected for the clustering and stratification that defined the sampling strategy.
report	V;PST;V.PTCP	reported	Thus, all estimates reported here can be considered nationally representative of the United States.
present	V;PRS;SG;3	presents	This section briefly presents the Gújjolaay Eegimaa (Eegimaa for short; Ethnologue code: ISO 639-3: bqj), its speakers and its varieties.
discuss	V;PRS;1;PL	discuss	We also discuss aspects of the contact situation of the Eegimaa language.
show	V;NFIN	show	This is important, because as we will show in § 6 below, some of the Eegimaa death prevention names and child-bearing names are given to Eegimaa people from other communities.
speak	V;PST;V.PTCP	spoken	Eegimaa is a Jóola language spoken in the Basse Casamance area of Southern Senegal by a population estimated to be between 7,000 (Bassène, 2007) and 11,200 speakers (Lewis, Simons, & Fennig, 2014).
classify	V;PST;V.PTCP	classified	Jóola languages are classified as members of Sapir’s (1971) BAK group of Atlantic languages of the Niger Congo language phylum.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	Speakers of these languages are found in the Gambia, in the former Casamance region of Southern Senegal and in Guinea Bissau.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	The map in Figure 1, taken from Ethnologue, presents the languages of Senegal.
point	V;PRS;SG;3	points	The arrow on the map points to the Eegimaa speaking area (Bandial is the name used by Ethnologue for Eegimaa).
speak	V;PST;V.PTCP	spoken	Eegimaa is mainly spoken in Mof-Ávvi, a former kingdom of 10 villages located to the west of Ziguinchor, the capital city of the former administrative region of Casamance in the south of Senegal.
border	V;PST;V.PTCP	bordered	Mof-Ávvi is a peninsula bordered by the Casamance River to the north, which separates it from the Jóola Búluf and Jóola Fogny areas, and to the east by the villages Brin and Djibonker, where the speakers of Kujireray and Baïnounk Gubëeher live.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	In the south, the river known as Kamobeul Bolon separates Mof-Ávvi from the Bayot speaking area, while the west side of Mof-Ávvi coincides with the homeland of the Jóola Kaasa Húluf and Jóola Kaasa Ésuulaaluʔ.
adapt	V;PST;V.PTCP	adapted	The map in Figure 2 below, adapted from Palmeri and Gazio (1995), shows a very approximate location of Mof-Ávvi in relation to other neighbouring speech communities.
refer	V;PST;V.PTCP	referred	Some of these communities will be referred to in our discussion of death prevention names in § 6.
speak	V;PST;V.PTCP	spoken	The Eegimaa spoken in Mof-Ávvi is known by several names given by speakers of neighbouring languages.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	For example, the name Kusiilay 1 ‘the language of Essil’, is the name given by speakers of Kujireray to the Eegimaa language, because Essil, of which Bajjat was a district, is the village which has a border with their village.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	Endungo is the name given by Bayot speakers, but its meaning is not known.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	Kúlaakiay, ‘the language of village of Seleki/Sállagi’, is the name given by Jóola Kaasa speakers who are more familiar with the village of Seleki/Sállagi’ than all other villages of Mof-Ávvi.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	Finally, Banjal/Bandial ‘the language of the village of Banjal’, the most popular of all those names, is the name given by the Jóola Búluf and Jóola Fogny speakers to Eegimaa people and their language, because people from the village of Banjal were the first to be in contact with them.
speak	V;PRS;V.PTCP	speaking	Map of the Eegimaa speaking area
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	Village names are written using their usual spelling.
provide	V;PST;V.PTCP	provided	The Ethnologue language codes are provided for language varieties that have them.
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	Language names are written following the recommendation for the transcription of national languages of Senegal, Decree 2005-981.
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	Names of the villages of Mof-Ávvi are written using the Eegimaa orthography and following the pronunciation of village names by native speakers.
include	V;PRS;3;PL	include	Jóola language varieties which are closely related to Eegimaa include Kujireray/Kuluunaay (from the village of Brin with 76% lexical similarity) and Gufiñamay (from the village of Afiniam with 74% lexical similarity (based on Lewis et al., 2014 eds.)).
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	They are located north of the Casamance River in the Jóola Búluf area.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	There are also small migrant Eegimaa communities in villages like Djifanghor (east of Ziguinchor) and in Bourofaye (south of Ziguinchor), where they cohabit with speakers of Baïnounk languages.
live	V;PRS;3;PL	live	Nowadays most Eegimaa speakers from Mof-Ávvi live outside their homeland, generally in urban areas like Ziguinchor and Dakar.
use	V;PRS;3;PL	use	Speakers of Eegimaa use the term Gújjolaay (meaning Jóola) to refer to their language when they speak to each other, but Eegimaa to distinguish their Jóola variety from that of other Jóola peoples.
decline	V;PRS;V.PTCP	declining	Eegimaa is an endangered language whose transmission to new generations has been declining significantly in the last decades.
understand	V;NFIN	understand	It is important to understand the contact situation of Eegimaa, because some of the ritual names analysed in this paper come from other languages or from neighbouring communities.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	These names are given either in Eegimaa or in a different language.
achieve	V;NFIN	achieve	After Bedřich Smetana, he was the next Czech Romantic-era composer to achieve worldwide recognition.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Following	Following Smetana's nationalist example, Dvořák frequently employed aspects, specifically rhythms, of the folk music of Moravia and his native Bohemia.
describe	V;PST;V.PTCP	described	Dvořák's own style has been described as "the fullest recreation of a national idiom with that of the symphonic tradition, absorbing folk influences and finding effective ways of using them". [1]
display	V;PST;SG;3	displayed	Dvořák displayed his musical gifts at an early age, being an apt violin student from age six.
seek	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Seeking	Seeking recognition beyond the Prague area, he submitted a score of his First Symphony to a prize competition in Germany, but did not win, and the unreturned manuscript was lost until rediscovered many decades later.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	In 1874 he made a submission to the Austrian State Prize for Composition, including scores of two further symphonies and other works.
lead	V;PRS;V.PTCP	leading	Although Dvořák was not aware of it, Johannes Brahms was the leading member of the jury and was highly impressed.
award	V;PST;V.PTCP	awarded	The prize was awarded to Dvořák in 1874 [a] and again in 1876 and in 1877, when Brahms and the prominent critic Eduard Hanslick, also a member of the jury, made themselves known to him.
recommend	V;PST;SG;3	recommended	Brahms recommended Dvořák to his publisher, Simrock, who soon afterward commissioned what became the Slavonic Dances, Op. 46.
praise	V;PST;V.PTCP	praised	These were highly praised by the Berlin music critic Louis Ehlert in 1878, the sheet music (of the original piano 4-hands version) had excellent sales, and Dvořák's international reputation was launched at last.
premiere	V;PST;V.PTCP	premiered	Dvořák's first piece of a religious nature, his setting of Stabat Mater, was premiered in Prague in 1880.
perform	V;PST;V.PTCP	performed	It was very successfully performed in London in 1883, leading to many other performances in the United Kingdom and United States. [2]
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	In his career, Dvořák made nine invited visits to England, often conducting performances of his own works.
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	His Seventh Symphony was written for London.
visit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Visiting	Visiting Russia in March 1890, he conducted concerts of his own music in Moscow and Saint Petersburg. [3]
appoint	V;PST;V.PTCP	appointed	In 1891 Dvořák was appointed as a professor at the Prague Conservatory.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	In 1890 – 91, he wrote his Dumky Trio, one of his most successful chamber music pieces.
move	V;PST;SG;3	moved	In 1892, Dvořák moved to the United States and became the director of the National Conservatory of Music of America in New York City.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	While in the United States, Dvořák wrote his two most successful orchestral works: the Symphony From the New World, which spread his reputation worldwide, [4] and his Cello Concerto, one of the most highly regarded of all cello concerti.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	He also wrote his most appreciated piece of chamber music, the American String Quartet, during this time.
increase	V;PRS;V.PTCP	increasing	But shortfalls in payment of his salary, along with increasing recognition in Europe and an onset of homesickness, led him to leave the United States and return to Bohemia in 1895.
have	V;PRS;3;PL	have	All of Dvořák's nine operas but his first have librettos in Czech and were intended to convey Czech national spirit, as were some of his choral works.
Teach	V;PST;SG;3	Taught	Among his smaller works, the seventh Humoresque and the song "Songs My Mother Taught Me" are also widely performed and recorded.
describe	V;PST;V.PTCP	described	He has been described as "arguably the most versatile ... composer of his time". [5]
specialize	V;PST;SG;3	specialized	Jens Otto Harry Jespersen or Otto Jespersen (Danish: [ʌtˢo ˈjɛsb̥ɐsn̩]; 16 July 1860 – 30 April 1943) was a Danish linguist who specialized in the grammar of the English language.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Otto Jespersen was born in Randers in Jutland.
inspire	V;PST;V.PTCP	inspired	He was inspired by the work of Danish philologist Rasmus Rask as a boy, and with the help of Rask's grammars taught himself some Icelandic, Italian, and Spanish.
enter	V;PST;SG;3	entered	He entered the University of Copenhagen in 1877 when he was 17, initially studying law but not forgetting his language studies.
shift	V;PST;SG;3	shifted	In 1881 he shifted his focus completely to languages, and in 1887 earned his master's degree in French, with English and Latin as his secondary languages.
support	V;PST;SG;3	supported	He supported himself during his studies through part-time work as a schoolteacher and as a shorthand reporter in the Danish parliament.
travel	V;PST;SG;3	traveled	In 1887 – 1888, he traveled to England, Germany and France, meeting linguists like Henry Sweet and Paul Passy and attending lectures at institutions like Oxford University.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Following	Following the advice of his mentor Vilhelm Thomsen, he returned to Copenhagen in August 1888 and began work on his doctoral dissertation on the English case system.
defend	V;PST;SG;3	defended	He successfully defended his dissertation in 1891.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	Jespersen was a professor of English at the University of Copenhagen from 1893 to 1925, and served as Rector of the university in 1920 – 21.
focus	V;PST;SG;3	focused	His early work focused primarily on language teaching reform and on phonetics, but he is best known for his later work on syntax and on language development.
advance	V;PST;SG;3	advanced	He advanced the theories of Rank and Nexus in Danish in two papers: Sprogets logik (1913) and De to hovedarter af grammatiske forbindelser (1921).
remove	V;PRS;SG;3	removes	Jespersen in this theory of ranks removes the parts of speech from the syntax, and differentiates between primaries, secondaries, and tertiaries; e.g. in "well honed phrase," "phrase" is a primary, this being defined by a secondary, "honed", which again is defined by a tertiary "well".
apply	V;PST;V.PTCP	applied	The term Nexus is applied to sentences, structures similar to sentences and sentences in formation, in which two concepts are expressed in one unit; e.g., it rained, he ran indoors.
qualify	V;PST;V.PTCP	qualified	This term is qualified by a further concept called a junction which represents one idea, expressed by means of two or more elements, whereas a nexus combines two ideas.
prove	V;PST;3;PL	proved	Junction and nexus proved valuable in bringing the concept of context to the forefront of the attention of the world of linguistics.
recognize	V;PST;V.PTCP	recognized	He was most widely recognized for some of his books.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	Language: Its Nature, Development and Origin (1922) is considered by many to be his masterpiece.
concentrate	V;PST;SG;3	concentrated	Modern English Grammar on Historical Principles (1909 – 1949), concentrated on morphology and syntax, and Growth and Structure of the English Language (1905) is a comprehensive view of English by someone with another native language, and still in print, over 70 years after his death and more than 100 years after publication.
publish	V;PST;SG;3	published	Late in his life he published Analytic Syntax (1937), in which he presents his views on syntactic structure using an idiosyncratic shorthand notation.
challenge	V;PST;SG;3	challenged	In The Philosophy of Grammar (1924) he challenged the accepted views of common concepts in Grammar and proposed corrections to the basic definitions of grammatical case, pronoun, object, voice etc., and developed further his notions of Rank and Nexus.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	In the 21st century this book is still used as one of the basic texts in modern Structural linguistics.
pioneer	V;PRS;V.PTCP	pioneering	Mankind, Nation and Individual: from a linguistic point of view (1925) is one of the pioneering works on Sociolinguistics.
visit	V;PST;SG;3	visited	Jespersen visited the United States twice:
lecture	V;PST;SG;3	lectured	he lectured at the Congress of Arts and Sciences in St. Louis in 1904, and in 1909 – 1910 he visited both the University of California and Columbia University.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	While in the U.S., he took occasion to study the country's educational system.
see	V;NFIN;2	see	His autobiography (see below) was published in English translation as recently as 1995.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	Jespersen was a proponent of phonosemanticism and wrote:
be	V;PRS;SG;3	Is	“Is there really much more logic in the opposite extreme which denies any kind of sound symbolism (apart from the small class of evident echoisms and ‘onomatopoeia’) and sees in our words only a collection of accidental and irrational associations of sound and meaning? ...
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is no denying that there are words which we feel instinctively to be adequate to express the ideas they stand for.”
remain	V;PST;SG;3	remained	After his retirement in 1925, Jespersen remained active in the international linguistic community.
continue	V;PRS;V.PTCP	continuing	In addition to continuing to write, he convened and chaired the first International Meeting on Linguistic Research in Geneva in 1930, and acted as president of the Fourth International Congress of Linguists in Copenhagen in 1936.
publish	V;PST;SG;3	published	He was an early supporter of the Esperanto offshoot Ido and in 1927 published his own project Novial.
work	V;PST;SG;3	worked	He also worked with the International Auxiliary Language Association.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	Jespersen received honorary degrees from Columbia University in New York (1910), St. Andrews University in Scotland (1925), and the Sorbonne in Paris (1927).
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	He was one of the first six international scholars to be elected as honorary members of the Linguistic Society of America.
teach	V;NFIN	teach	Are you — do they still teach at Bahia on Sunday?
go	V;PST;V.PTCP	gone	In fact I would have gone today, if it wasn't for my back.
find	V;PST;SG;1	found	Well I finally found some people there I can dance with.
help	V;PRS;SG;3	helps	So that helps.
need	V;NFIN	need	Do you need a partner?
go	V;NFIN	go	To go there?
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	Besides, you, I mean if you're a guy, if —
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	If you're a woman, I mean, those guys'll be all over you.
swear	V;PRS;SG;1	swear	I swear it.
say	V;IMP;2	say	Don't say that, Harold's in the room.
run	V;IMP;2	Run	Run along now, dear.
come	V;NFIN	come	You could come too.
mean	V;NFIN	mean	Well I don't necessarily mean, in a bad way, even though I'm sure there are a few there, I'm not dancing with guys.
say	V;NFIN	say	All I can say is, the women aren't all over me.
remember	V;PRS;SG;1	remember	But, but I remember, like I went there with this person, it's kind of funny.
want	V;NFIN	want	Th- this person did not want to dance.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	So she's just gonna watch.
get	V;NFIN	get	So, every once in a while I'd get up and dance, and it's like, when I'd come back, I mean there was some guy there, sitting in my chair.
get	V;PST;SG;1	got	Every time I got up.
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	I'm thinking, God these guys don't waste any time.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, you turn your back and there's somebody moving in.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean I don't know if they —
come	V;NFIN	come	Maybe Harold you should come with me.
learn	V;IMP;2	Learn	Learn some lam- lambada.
find	V;NFIN	find	So I can find — find some girls with empty chairs next to em?
go	V;NFIN	go	Maybe I should go with Miles.
know	V;NFIN	know	Well I don't know if I told you, told you that story about that woman, who uh, went after that guy there?
tell	V;PST;SG;1	told	I told you that story, right?
sit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sitting	Uh, after the class is over w- over with, I'm sitting down at a table, there's a woman from — who was in the class, and two men, sitting to my right, is a table with two women there.
come	V;PST;V.PTCP	come	They had apparently just come from Oba Oba.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Oh I think you told me.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	And like, well they were attractive women, the one had on a real real short skirt.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	Anyway, there was this guy in his fifties, out there dancing, by himself.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	looking	And the woman was just looking at him, making eyes, then she went out there, got on her knees, in front of him, in this skirt that's this short, takes her hands, and goes along his toes, and then up, and is just doing like that.
figure	V;PST;SG;1	figured	I figured, oh, they must know each other.
assume	V;PST;SG;1	assumed	That's what I assumed.
do	V;PST;3;PL	did	And, they did that for the whole dance, and I mean, she was d- all kind of suggestive stuff, and touching him and everything, people would like cheering, and clapping, "go for it".
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	The – the song ended, the guy set down, at another table.
sit	V;PST;SG;3	set	And she set down —
dance	V;PST;3;PL	danced	They never danced again the rest of the evening.
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	This is like — so I was thinking, this woman's sitting about as far away from me as Jamie is.
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	And I was thinking, hm, this is interesting.
wonder	V;PRS;SG;1	wonder	I wonder how long it was gonna take the guys in this club to move in on her.
think	V;NFIN	think	No sooner did I think this thought, guys were over there.
ask	V;NFIN	ask	What, you didn't ask her to dance?
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	Well I was still trying to figure out, surely they must know each other, I mean, why wasn't he sitting at her table?
mean	V;PRS;2;SG	mean	You mean, you were still thinking about it.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	Anyway, this guy, I mean he comes over there, immediately afterwards —
get	V;PST;SG;3	got	The same guy who got his — the same guy who's —
sit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sitting	No, a random guy, who was like sitting about where you are.
sit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sitting	He was sitting there, there were two guys sitting at a table right where you are.
sit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sitting	And then these two women are sitting here.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	So uh, he comes over there, and is talking with that woman I don't know about what, but then like ten minutes later, she and her friend are over at their table.
know	V;PRS;2;PL	know	You know, kissing, et cetera, et cetera.
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	And I was thinking, it looks like these people aren't going home alone tonight.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	But I just thought that —
forbid	V;PRS;3;PL	forbid	No wonder they forbid this dance.
go	V;NFIN	go	Well, maybe I shouldn't go there then.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	Sam has been, has taken such an interest in this retirement bit that it — it really surprises me.
begin	V;PST;V.PTCP	begun	Well she's begun to listen.
know	V;PRS;2;SG	know	You know.
use	V;PST;SG;3	used	Uh, she used to go over and read a book or something.
turn	V;NFIN;2	turn	Yeah, or turn a deaf ear.
understand	V;PRS;2;SG	understand	But uh, once you understand it, you know, you, you're interested in getting right in, and going after it.
call	V;NFIN	call	I really oughta call, at least Reg Barr, and ask if uh, he's still speaking to me.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	Well, he came over to you all smiles, I noticed.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Well, we've known each other for thirty-some years, why wouldn't he?
open	V;PST;SG;3	opened	But a- anyway, um, I was so glad that he opened up on this disclosure bit.
object	V;PRS;V.PTCP	objecting	Because it was Burns that's been objecting to our having it.
know	V;PST;SG;1	knew	And I knew this.
open	V;PST;SG;3	opened	So when he opened up on that, and she followed through, and told about her husband, you know?
approve	V;NFIN	approve	Not necessarily that she would approve of it, but at least she wasn't disapproving.
get	V;NFIN	get	And I – I cannot get over this.
know	V;PRS;2;SG	know	And you know, he must have been thinking, when he said he wasn't a political appointee, he must have been associating his relationship with Steitler.
believe	V;NFIN	believe	I really do believe that.
know	V;NFIN	know	I don't know how else he could have done it.
topple	V;PRS;SG;3	topples	That topples over.
say	V;NFIN	say	Um, but he didn't say that.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	He said, I loved your hum- humor, uh, and I love you, but, I am not a political appointee.
snap	V;PRS;V.PTCP	snapping	And his eyes were just snapping.
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	And I said, Oh? How did you get on the board?
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Well he said, I got a letter from the governor, asking.
mention	V;PST;V.PTCP	mentioned	The point was, I could have mentioned what was in the paper about him, that he was related and, you know, had business with Steitler, and I just said, and there's Doctor Duvall, you know?
go	V;PST;SG;1	went	And then I went to Rotus, who's a seasoned politician, if there ever was one.
flare	V;NFIN	flare	He just would flare.
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	And I said, well, I – alright.
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	I said, I'll apologize next time, and I'm gonna make hay out of it, don't you worry.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	wan	I wanna know how you birds got on that board, if you're not political appointees.
grow	V;NFIN	grow	Can I grow some basil?
have	V;PRS;SG;1	've	Yes, that's how I've –
have	V;NFIN	have	I don't have any this year, but I've grown it other years.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's no proble-.
take	V;NFIN	take	It will not take any frost.
learn	V;PST;SG;1	learned	I l- I learned that one time the –
learn	V;PST;SG;1	learned	I learned it just went down.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Well, I think it was Barbara that has some seeds.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	It comes right up.
give	V;PST;2;SG	gave	Isn't that what you gave the neighbor one time?
give	V;PST;2;SG	gave	You gave him some kind of herb.
give	V;NFIN	give	Did I give him some?
give	V;PST;SG;1	gave	I gave him a red pepper.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think you – I think you gave him some herb of some kind.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	I may have given – given him some basil, yes.
have	V;NFIN	have	I don't have any this year, I forgot to plant it.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	I have oregano.
want	V;PRS;2;PL	want	A good stand of it, if you want any.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	I have a lot of dried or- oregano.
smoke	V;PST;2;SG	smoked	You smoked it down into the cork, didn't you?
smoke	V;PST;2;SG	smoked	You smoked it down into the cork.
like	V;NFIN	like	Well, yeah, you don't like that, do you, but –
choke	V;PRS;SG;3	chokes	No, it chokes me to death.
smoke	V;IMP;2	smoke	It's – it's there, might as well smoke it.
please	V;NFIN	please	Mr. Chief Justice, may it please the Court:
arrest	V;PST;V.PTCP	arrested	In September 1975 the Petitioner was arrested when he tried to steal a coat from a department store.
bring	V;PST;V.PTCP	brought	Six months later he was brought to trial on a charge of attempted petty larceny.
plead	V;PST;V.PTCP	pled	Had Petitioner pled guilty to that offense or been convicted of it after trial, he could have received a maximum sentence of one year in jail.
release	V;PST;V.PTCP	released	He would have been released, at the very latest, in September of 1976.
plead	V;NFIN	plead	But Petitioner did not plead guilty and was not convicted.
raise	V;PST;SG;3	raised	Instead, he raised an insanity defense, claiming that because he was mentally ill at the time he tried to steal the coat he should not be blamed for his act.
contest	V;NFIN	contest	The Government did not contest this claim and the trial court found, after a stipulated trial, that the Petitioner was not guilty by reason of insanity.
order	V;PST;V.PTCP	ordered	On the basis of this acquittal and on this basis alone, the Petitier was -- Petitioner was ordered committed indefinitely to a mental hospital.
order	V;PST;V.PTCP	ordered	That is, he was ordered confined until he could prove that he was not mentally ill and dangerous.
remain	V;PRS;SG;3	remains	P- Petitioner still remains confined in the hospital on the basis of that commitment, even though more than seven years have now passed since his initial incarceration and more than six years have passed since the time he would have necessarily been released had he been convicted rather than acquitted.
remain	V;NFIN	remain	Conceivably, he will remain committed in the hospital for the rest of his life.
contend	V;PRS;SG;3	contends	Petitioner contends that whatever the validity of his kinitial commitment to the hospital, once he had been confined there for longer than he could have been incarcerated upon conviction, his commitment became an indefinite one.
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	And thus, the Government was constitutionally required to prove the need for his commitment by clear and convincing evidence, something which the Government has yet to do with respect to Mr. Jones.
do	V;NFIN	do	If it cannot do so now, Petitioner submits, he is entitled to release.
challenge	V;PRS;V.PTCP	challenging	Mr. Wasserstrom, you're not challenging, as I understand it, the -- the initial commitment.
understand	V;PRS;SG;1	understand	The only question, if I understand your position correctly, that you raise is whether he must be released at the time that the sentence period would have expired under the criminal statute, is that right?
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Our position is, Your Honor, that he has to be released at that time unless the Government can at that time prove his commitability by the standards required.
challenge	V;PRS;V.PTCP	challenging	And you are not challenging, then, the, uh, standard or burden of proof at the time of his commitment?
raise	V;PST;V.PTCP	raised	It's our position that that question is not raised in this case.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	Um, there are arguable justifications for that initial commitment, and we don't discuss one way or another whether those justifications are sufficient to validate that initial commitment and we don't think the Court need reach that question.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	Whatever reasons there are that might have init- -- have vali- -- have justified his initial commitment, those reasons would have justified only a commitment that could have lasted, without the Government meeting a burden of proof, for as long as he could have been incarcerated upon conviction --
serve	V;NFIN	serve	If his initial commitment is not punitive, then how is the statutory period of, uh, time that he could serve on the criminal charge relevant?
think	V;PRS;1;PL	think	Well, we think the only justifications that could, the only justifications that could, um, that could countenance that initial commitment are punitive in nature, that is, are a kind of punitive rationale.
agree	V;NFIN	agree	And so, to -- for us to agree with your position we would have to conclude that his initial commitment was punitive?
think	V;NFIN	think	Well, Your Honor, I don't think that it's terribly important whether the word "punitive" is used, but I do think that any kinds of justifications which would justify the initial commitment are what I would call backward looking justifications, that is, justifications that do turn on the fact that he, it -- was found to have committed a criminal act beyond a reasonable doubt.
treat	V;PST;V.PTCP	treated	And whether they're treated as purely punitive or not doesn't seem to me is, is, is, is important, or the label that we attach to them it doesn't seem to me is important.
turn	V;NFIN	turn	The point is, though, those rationales all do turn on the nature of his act, and his act is one which, we submit, justifies confinement only for a year.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	But hasn't this Court, as well as the United States Court of Appeals in the D.C. Circuit, said there's no rational connection between the possible sentence and the possible length of stay after an acquittal, not guilty by reason of insanity?
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	I believe that the law in the United States Court of Appeals in Brown against the United States and in Wade v. Jacobs, in both of those cases the Court suggested that there is a relationship between the sentence that might have been imposed and the length that the commitment can persist without the Government proving, uh, proving commitability under civil commitment standards.
think	V;NFIN	think	I don't think that this Court has, has uh, has addressed that question, and I then think this is the first case that raises it.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	Oh, the Court has said expressly that there's no connection, in one case some time ago.
excuse	V;IMP;2	excuse	Well, excuse me.
suggest	V;PST;SG;3	suggested	Well, it is true in Lynch v. Overholser this Court suggested certain, uh, rationales for a commitment, but it didn't then go on to say whether those rationales were ones which would justify an indefinite commitment or simply one that would persist as long as the sentence that might have been imposed.
call	V;PRS;2;SG	call	And it's our view that those rationales, whether you call them punitive or not, are, are all ones which evaporate once the accou- -- once the insanity acquittee has been confined for as long as he could have been confined if he had been convicted.
depend	V;NFIN	depend	Does your position depend at all on whether or not the initial crime was a crime of violence?
suppose	V;IMP;2	suppose	Uh, suppose this had been a street mugging, for example?
impose	V;PST;V.PTCP	imposed	Well, of course, the more serious the crime, the longer the maximum sentence that could have been imposed and the more distant, uh, the rights that we're talking about, the more distant in time the rights we're talking about would become available.
please	V;NFIN	please	Chief Justice Kennedy and may it please the Court.
argue	V;PRS;V.PTCP	arguing	My name is Eric Levi and I'm arguing for appellant Darnell Hurt.
argue	V;PRS;V.PTCP	arguing	Uh, attorney John Martin will be arguing the rebuttal, and we'd reserve five minutes of time.
like	V;NFIN	like	Your honors, if I may, I'd like to start by addressing proposition of law number three.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	In proposition of law number three, we have an ultimate finding of fact which was resolved in favor of Darnell Hurt.
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	Darnell Hurt had asked for the provocation mitiger- mitigator to apply to all offenses which he was charged, which includes the felonious assault, and indirectly then he also requested it, um, for the felony murder charge as well.
believe	V;PRS;1;PL	believe	We believe that on retrial, collateral estoppel would prevent retrial without regard for the jury's prior finding in Hurt's favor, that ther- that the provocation mitigator existed, that he was acting under serious provocation.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	In fact, at the trial, the State, and it can be found in the transcript at pages 774 and 775, admitted as much and conceded so in its closing argument by stating to the jury that the victim was coming into this apartment.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	He was coming in hot and he pulled out his firearm.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	And here comes Melvin Dobson arriving at the scene to the rescue of his daughter.
incense	V;PRS;SG;3	incenses	And that incenses him, Hurt, to the point where he uses deadly force.
say	V;PST;3;PL	said	And then they said, if you apply all the factors, that's voluntary manslaughter.
apply	V;PRS;SG;3	applies	That's where this count applies.
direct	V;PST;V.PTCP	directed	The State of Ohio had then directed the jury to find these provocation mitigators, which it did.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	looking	In looking at Green versus United States and the courts, the high court out of the state of Texas and the federal circuits in Kennedy versus Washington on this constitutional issue, I believe that the authority uh exists to find that collateral estoppel would preclude the defendant from having to prove these mitigators a second time to a different jury because he preserved the issue by requesting the jury instruction for the mitigators on all of the applicable counts, and he didn't request that the jury -- or concede to permit the jury to be discharged without considering those mitigators, when the jury didn't consider them and the additional counts, it acted almost as an applied acquittal or an applied um finding towards this affirmative defense.
agree	V;PST;SG;3	agreed	The high court in Texas in ex parte Jimmy Dean Watkins agreed with that.
indicate	V;PST;SG;3	indicated	And it indicated that in any additional trial, once a jury finds those provocation mitigators, that it can't be required to find them again.
state	V;PST;SG;3	stated	Additionally, in the Kennedy versus Washington uh matter, the Federal Circuit Court stated that the Green versus United States implied acquittal would generally apply in cases where the provocation mitigator was at issue.
address	V;PST;V.PTCP	addressed	It was addressed and argued by both sides, and the jury was asked to make a finding.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	Here the jury made the finding.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	It made a specific finding for the provocation mitigator.
find	V;NFIN	find	And while it didn't find it on each and every specific count, that was solely because the instruction wasn't given even though requested.
relate	V;PST;V.PTCP	related	Counsel, um, shouldn't the arguments related to Proposition three B, are they ripe for for ruling?
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Shouldn't these arguments be made at the -- at the trial court, given the fact that this was remanded?
remand	V;PST;V.PTCP	remanded	So, Your Honor, this was remanded for a retrial and it's remanded on the grounds of double jeopardy through the prong of collateral estoppel.
preclude	V;PST;V.PTCP	precluded	And under double jeopardy, not only is an individual precluded from being convicted twice, or to stand trial twice for an offense, he was already acquitted of, he's also precluded from having to actually stand trial to go back to trial for a second time when the acquittal was already made.
amount	V;PRS;SG;3	amounts	In this case, it's a finding of fact under collateral estoppel, but it amounts to an affirmative defense to acquit him of the murder, essentially.
negate	V;PST;V.PTCP	negated	Um, so where the murderer was already negated by the findings for the -- the mitigators, we believe that it is ripe at this point um prior to new trial, that this court rule on this and preclude um the trial court from requiring that he face the murder charges again.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, how can they be ripe?
excuse	V;NFIN;2	excuse	The proble-, uh, excuse me, the uh -- the self defense issues uh we believe are ripe first and foremost in proposition.
manipulate	V;PRS;SG;3	Manipulates	41: The Politics of Fear: How It Manipulates Us to Tribalism (Javanbakht)
walk	V;PRS;SG;3	walks	President Barack Obama walks away from the lectern with Vice President Joe Biden after making a statement about the mass shooting in Orlando, Fla., June 16, 2016.
kill	V;PRS;V.PTCP	killing	The cruel murder of 50 people in New Zealand was another tragic reminder of how humans are capable of heartlessly killing their own kind just based on what they believe, how they worship, and what race or nationality they belong to.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a longstanding history of the fear of “the others” turning humans into illogical ruthless weapons, in service to an ideology.
ingrain	V;PST;V.PTCP	ingrained	It is deeply ingrained in the living organisms that have survived extinction through billions of years of evolution.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Demagogues have always used fear for intimidation of the subordinates or enemies, and shepherding the tribe by the leaders.
blur	V;NFIN	blur	Fear is a very strong tool that can blur humans’ logic and change their behavior.
specialize	V;PRS;V.PTCP	specializing	I am a psychiatrist and neuroscientist specializing in fear and trauma, and I have some evidence-based thoughts on how fear is abused in politics.
learn	V;PRS;1;PL	learn	We learn fear from tribe mates
learn	V;NFIN	learn	Like other animals, we humans can learn fear from experience, such as being attacked by a predator.
learn	V;PRS;1;PL	learn	We also learn from observation, such as witnessing a predator attacking another human.
learn	V;PRS;1;PL	learn	And, we learn by instructions, such as being told there is a predator nearby.
learn	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Learning	Learning from our conspecifics – members of the same species – is an evolutionary advantage that has prevented us from repeating dangerous experiences of other humans.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have a tendency to trust our tribe mates and authorities, especially when it comes to danger.
tell	V;PST;3;PL	told	It is adaptive: Parents and wise old men told us not to eat a special plant, or not to go to an area in the woods, or we would be hurt.
trust	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trusting	By trusting them, we would not die like a great-grandfather who died eating that plant.
accumulate	V;PST;1;PL	accumulated	This way we accumulated knowledge.
be	V;PST;V.PTCP	been	There has always been competition between groups of humans in different ways and with different faces, from brutal wartime nationalism to a strong loyalty to a football team.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	Evidence from cultural neuroscience shows that our brains even respond differently at an unconscious level simply to the view of faces from other races or cultures.
pray	V;PRS;3;PL	pray	At a tribal level, people are more emotional and consequently less logical: Fans of both teams pray for their team to win, hoping God will take sides in a game.
regress	V;PRS;1;PL	regress	On the other hand, we regress to tribalism when afraid.
lead	V;NFIN	lead	This is an evolutionary advantage that would lead to the group cohesion and help us fight the other tribes to survive.
bank	V;PST;V.PTCP	banked	Tribalism is the biological loophole that many politicians have banked on for a long time: tapping into our fears and tribal instincts.
give	V;NFIN	give	The typical pattern is to give the other humans a different label than us, and say they are going to harm us or our resources, and to turn the other group into a concept.
have	V;NFIN	have	It does not have to necessarily be race or nationality, which are used very often.
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	The list goes on and on.
build	V;PRS;V.PTCP	building	When building tribal boundaries between “us” and “them,” some politicians have managed very well to create virtual groups of people that do not communicate and hate without even knowing each other: This is the human animal in action!
start	V;PRS;3;PL	start	Very often my patients with phobias start with: “I know it is stupid, but I am afraid of spiders.”
reply	V;PRS;SG;1	reply	And I always reply: “It is not stupid, it is illogical.”
have	V;PRS;3;PL	have	We humans have different functions in the brain, and fear oftentimes bypasses logic.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	There are several reasons.
run	V;IMP;2	run	In situations of danger, we ought to be fast: First run or kill, then think.
use	V;PRS;3;PL	use	Politicians and the media very often use fear to circumvent our logic.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	I always say the U.S. media are disaster pornographers – they work too much on triggering their audiences’ emotions.
surprise	V;PRS;V.PTCP	surprising	They are kind of political reality shows, surprising to many from outside the U.S.
kill	V;PRS;SG;3	kills	When one person kills a few others in a city of millions, which is of course a tragedy, major networks’ coverage could lead one to perceive the whole city is under siege and unsafe.
murder	V;PRS;SG;3	murders	If one undocumented illegal immigrant murders a U.S. citizen, some politicians use fear with the hope that few will ask: “This is terrible, but how many people were murdered in this country by U.S. citizens just today?”
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	Or: “I know several murders happen every week in this town, but why am I so scared now that this one is being showcased by the media?”
ask	V;NFIN	ask	We do not ask these questions, because fear bypasses logic.
turn	V;NFIN	turn	Fear can turn violent
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a reason that the response to fear is called the “fight or flight” response.
help	V;PST;V.PTCP	helped	That response has helped us survive the predators and other tribes that have wanted to kill us.
abuse	V;PST;V.PTCP	abused	But again, it is another loophole in our biology to be abused to turn on our aggression toward “the others,” whether in the form of vandalizing their temples or harassing them on the social media.
desecrate	V;PST;V.PTCP	Desecrated	“Desecrated Graves in the Old Jewish Section of the Cemetery, Vienna” by Scott Rettberg is licensed under CC BY 2.0
manage	V;PRS;3;PL	manage	When ideologies manage to get hold of our fear circuitry, we often regress to illogical, tribal and aggressive human animals, becoming weapons ourselves – weapons that politicians use for their own agenda.
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	This essay originally appeared in The Conversation.
manipulate	V;PRS;SG;3	Manipulates	The Politics of Fear: How It Manipulates Us to Tribalism by Arash Javanbakht is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 3.0 International License.
fix	V;IMP;2	Fix	Fix the Machine, Not the Person (Swartz)
admit	V;PRS;SG;3	admits	“Everything was a fight,” the head of the union admits.
spend	V;PST;3;PL	spent	“They spent more time on grievances and on things like that than they did on producing cars.
have	V;PST;3;PL	had	They had strikes all the time.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	It was considered the worst workforce in the automobile industry in the United States.”
buy	V;NFIN	buy	“One of the expressions was, you can buy anything you want in the GM plant in Fremont,” adds Jeffrey Liker, a professor who studied the plant.
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	“If you want sex, if you want drugs, if you want alcohol, it's there.
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	During breaks, during lunch time, if you want to gamble illegally—any illegal activity was available for the asking within that plant.”
have	V;NFIN	have	Absenteeism was so bad that some mornings they didn’t have enough employees to start the assembly line; they had to go across the street and drag people out of the bar.
try	V;PST;SG;3	tried	When management tried to punish workers, workers tried to punish them right back: scratching cars, loosening parts in hard-to-reach places, filing union grievances, sometimes even building cars unsafely.
close	V;PST;SG;3	closed	In 1982, GM finally closed the plant.
plan	V;PRS;V.PTCP	planning	But the very next year, when Toyota was planning to start its first plant in the US, it decided to partner with GM to reopen it, hiring back the same old disastrous workers into the very same jobs.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	And so began the most fascinating experiment in management history.
flead	V;PST;SG;3	flew	Toyota flew this rowdy crew to Japan, to see an entirely different way of working: The Toyota Way.
consider	V;PST;3;PL	considered	At Toyota, labor and management considered themselves on the same team; when workers got stuck, managers didn’t yell at them, but asked how they could help and solicited suggestions.
have	V;PST;2;SG	had	“You had union workers—grizzled old folks that had worked on the plant floor for 30 years, and they were hugging their Japanese counterparts, just absolutely in tears,” recalls their Toyota trainer.
sound	V;NFIN	sound	“And it might sound flowery to say 25 years later, but they had had such a powerful emotional experience of learning a new way of working, a way that people could actually work together collaboratively — as a team.
get	V;PST;3;PL	got	Three months after they got back to the US and reopened the plant, everything had changed.
fall	V;PST;3;PL	fell	Grievances and absenteeism fell away and workers started saying they actually enjoyed coming to work.
skyrocket	V;PST;V.PTCP	skyrocketed	The Fremont factory, once one of the worst in the US, had skyrocketed to become the best.
make	V;PST;3;PL	made	The cars they made got near-perfect quality ratings.
make	V;NFIN	make	And the cost to make them had plummeted.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	It's almost like a machine made of men and women.
think	V;IMP;2	Think	Think of an assembly line.
take	V;PST;2;SG	took	If you just took a bunch of people and threw them in a warehouse with a bunch of car parts and a manual, it’d probably be a disaster.
build	V;PST;V.PTCP	built	Instead, a careful structure has been built: car parts roll down on a conveyor belt, each worker does one step of the process, everything is carefully designed and routinized.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	And when the system isn’t working, it doesn’t make sense to just yell at the people in it—any more than you’d try to fix a machine by yelling at the gears.
have	V;PRS;2;SG	have	True, sometimes you have the wrong gears and need to replace them, but more often you’re just using them in the wrong way.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	’s	When there’s a problem, you shouldn’t get angry with the gears—you should fix the machine.
have	V;PRS;2;SG	have	If you have goals in life, you’re probably going to need some sort of organization.
think	V;NFIN	think	Even if it’s an organization of just you, it’s still helpful to think of it as a kind of machine.
need	V;NFIN	need	You don’t need to do every part of the process yourself—you just need to set up the machine so that the right outcomes happen.
let	V;IMP;2	let	For example, let’s say you want to build a treehouse in the backyard.
saw	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sawing	You’re great at sawing and hammering, but architecture is not your forte.
build	V;PRS;2;SG	build	You build and build, but the treehouses keep falling down.
try	V;NFIN	try	Sure, you can try to get better at architecture, develop a better design, but you can also step back, look at the machine as a whole, and decide to fire yourself as the architect.
find	V;PRS;2;SG	find	Instead, you find a friend who loves that sort of thing to design the treehouse for you and you stick to actually building it.
build	V;NFIN	build	After all, your goal was to build a treehouse whose design you like—does it really matter whether you’re the one who actually designed it?
let	V;IMP;2	let	Or let’s say you really want to get in shape, but never remember to exercise.
keep	V;NFIN	keep	You can keep beating yourself up for your forgetfulness, or you can put a system in place.
have	V;PRS;2;SG	have	Maybe you have your roommate check to see that you exercise before you leave your house in the morning or you set a regular time to consistently go to the gym together.
have	V;NFIN	have	Life isn’t a high school exam; you don’t have to solve your problems on your own.
gather	V;PST;3;PL	gathered	In 1967, Edward Jones and Victor Harris gathered a group of college students and asked them to judge another student’s exam (the student was a fictional character, but let’s call him Jim).
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	The exam always had one question, asking Jim to write an essay on Fidel Castro “as if [he] were giving the opening statement in a debate.”
move	V;PRS;V.PTCP	moving	You’re on the 236th-level Kaiserstrasse moving sidewalk when you see her.
lean	V;PRS;V.PTCP	leaning	You’re leaning on the railing, waiting to ask Derya about a job, watching the glittering stream of mites that arc over half the sky — flying up to rewind their nanosprings in the stratospheric sunlight, flying down to make Frankfurt run.
get	V;PRS;2;SG	get	You never get tired of watching them.
hang	V;PST;V.PTCP	hung	Someone’s hung it up here — a hundred meters of clean gray and green twentieth-century modernism, plucked up from the river Main and suspended in the chilly air 2,360 meters up, between a lump of wooded parkland and a cluster of antique subway cars.
wear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	wearing	She’s wearing a 1950s sundress and a broad-brimmed hat, and it’s like an essay on the last century — the austere steel bridge, the bright blobs of subway graffiti, and her yellow dress, flapping against her legs as she climbs over the bridge’s rail.
hang	V;PRS;V.PTCP	hanging	She’s beyond the rail now, hanging out over the mountain - high drop.
move	V;PRS;3;PL	move	Thin translucent shadows move across her — the shadows of the neosilk-and-nanotube filaments that hang the city from the hundreds of five-kilometer-high towers that encircle it.
notice	V;PRS;SG;3	notices	(A civic agent notices you noticing, and attaches itself to your infospace, whispering statistics —
weather	V;NFIN	weather	“Each object’s suspension must weather a class 5 hurricane and the destruction of 80% of the towers” —
brush	V;PRS;2;SG	brush	until you brush it away.)
watch	V;PRS;V.PTCP	watching	You’re watching her lean out.
whip	V;PRS;SG;3	whips	The wind whips her hair, ruffles the skirt around her knees.
remember	V;PRS;2;SG	remember	You remember your first trip to the upper levels: leaning over the edge into the angry swarm of mites, whirring and buzzing warnings and shoving you back like a million mosquito chaperones.
try	V;PRS;SG;3	tries	Everyone tries it once — Except that there aren’t any mites around her.
clutch	V;PRS;2;SG	clutch	You clutch the railing.
surge	V;PRS;SG;3	surges	Hot, animal fear surges in your chest.
look	V;PRS;SG;3	looks	She looks up at you and, across the gap of forty meters, smiles a brilliant, heartbreaking smile.
let	V;PRS;SG;3	lets	Then she lets go and falls.
scream	V;PRS;2;SG	scream	You scream.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“Fucking airsurfers,” says Derya.
step	V;PRS;SG;3	steps	He steps off the moving sidewalk near you: tall, hook-nosed, the fashionable whorls of pox and acne making constellations of his cheeks and chest; the glowing, formal tattoos of his committees and lifebrands adorning his massive triceps.
swallow	V;PRS;2;SG	swallow	You swallow on a dry throat.
hear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	hearing	Derya, of all people, hearing you scream!
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	He gives you a hooded look.
infect	V;PRS;3;PL	infect	“They infect themselves with some designer virus -
let	V;PRS;SG;3	lets	it lets them hack the city’s person-recognition systems.
see	V;NFIN	see	So the mites don’t see them when they jump.
watch	V;IMP;2	Watch	Watch —”
sweep	V;PST;V.PTCP	swept	She’s swept past the whalelike oval of the public pool on the 202nd, past the sloping mandala of the Google of offices on the 164th.
hang	V;PST;V.PTCP	hung	At the 131st, below her, is the old Stock Exchange, hung upside-down now as a hipster den.
close	V;PRS;V.PTCP	closing	Now the mites are finally closing in.
coalesce	V;PRS;SG;3	coalesces	A silver swarm coalesces around the 164th, and she vanishes into it, like a snip of scallion into cloudy miso soup.
disperse	V;PRS;SG;3	disperses	When the cloud disperses, she’s standing on one of the Stock Exchange’s upside-down overhangs.
wave	V;PRS;SG;3	waves	She waves, antlike, then crawls through a dormer window.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“It’s not funny,” Derya says.
cause	V;PRS;V.PTCP	causing	Ripple effects are causing project slowdowns ...”
drive	V;PRS;3;PL	drive	“Freeloaders drive systemic evolution,” you find yourself saying.
quote	V;NFIN	quote	“Don’t you quote the founders at me,” Derya snaps.
find	V;PRS;3;PL	find	The minute enough people find anticontributive behavior cool, the party’s over —
stare	V;PRS;SG;3	stares	He stares until you meet his eye.
talk	V;PRS;2;SG	talk	“You even talk to those people -
pay	V;PRS;V.PTCP	paying	are you paying attention? —
talk	V;PRS;2;SG	talk	you even talk to them, your rep will be trashed on all the major servers.
work	V;NFIN	work	You won’t work, you won’t party, you’ll be defriended by every one of your tribes.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	Got	Got it?”
sail	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sailing	Her broad-brimmed hat is still sailing on the wind.
miss	V;PST;3;PL	missed	The mites missed it.
cut	V;PRS;SG;3	cuts	It cuts between the towers of the 50th.
desert	V;PST;V.PTCP	deserted	The upside-down trading floor is deserted.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	There are heaps of yellowed euros and deutschmarks dumped here, like snowdrifts.
realize	V;PRS;2;SG	realize	You realize: no mites.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The city has no eyes or ears here.
walk	V;PRS;2;SG	walk	You walk through empty, miteless rooms, stepping around light fixtures.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	She comes to you.
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	“You want it?” she says.
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	“You want to be infected?
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	You want to fly?”
nod	V;PRS;2;SG	nod	You nod.
close	V;PRS;V.PTCP	closing	Eyes closing, she leans in for the kiss.
want	V;PST;SG;3	wanted	Steven wanted to be a dentist.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	It was pretty much the first thing he told us when, as first years, we were all bunched together in the unfamiliar dusty classroom.
invite	V;PST;V.PTCP	invited	When, a couple of weeks later we were invited over to his house we saw that this was not an idle remark.
decorate	V;PST;V.PTCP	decorated	Where our bedroom walls were decorated with posters of pop stars and footballers, Steven had diagrams setting out tooth implementations, anatomical drawings of the jaw with brightly coloured nerves, muscle, blood vessels, bones, and, of course, teeth.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	Where we had shelves of plastic dinosaurs and superhero figurines from cornflake boxes, Steven had plaster casts of jawbones, gums and teeth grinning down at us.
lock	V;PST;V.PTCP	locked	The instruments were locked away in glass cabinets and only his parents had the key.
encourage	V;NFIN	encourage	“They don’t exactly encourage my interest,” he shrugged.
tolerate	V;PRS;3;PL	tolerate	“They sort of tolerate it.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	But Dad says they’re a sort of collection, and too expensive for me to play with.”
try	V;PST;1;PL	tried	Of course, we tried to pick the locks, but the spikes and saws and drillheads remained — tantalisingly — out of reach.
see	V;NFIN	see	We didn’t see Steven’s interest as strange, well, not that much.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	We all had our own interests — drawing, electronics, collecting beer caps — that each appeared obscure to anyone else.
cross	V;PST;V.PTCP	crossed	DIY Dentistry was just something that had never crossed our horizon before.
see	V;NFIN	see	And if Steven didn’t see it as weird, or no weirder than Andy showing us his Eastern-Block beer caps all laid out neatly on cork boards with little hand-written labels, why should it bother us?
suppose	V;PRS;SG;1	suppose	I suppose that, in our own way, we were all collectively strange.
pick	V;PST;SG;3	picked	The bunch that no one picked for their side at football or hockey, and that was the glue that held us together.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Besides, Steven had some really good stuff: sweets that stained your teeth bright red — well, technically, not your teeth, just the plaque, but the result was often the same.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	It took a few days good brushing to get it off.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	He had effervescent tablets that turned the canteen drinking water into that exact same pink mixture that the dentist gave you to rinse your mouth out after scraping around inside, and guaranteed to make the other kids splutter and spit it out when the taste slowly percolated through and met the memory of their last checkup.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	Then there was the event that became the rite of passage into our group of misfits and outcasts: making a mould of your teeth.
see	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeing	It was inevitable that one of us, seeing the plaster casts on the shelf by Steven’s bed would ask how they were done.
mix	V;PRS;V.PTCP	mixing	It was just as inevitable that a few minutes later, Steven would be mixing up various powders and liquids, and pouring them into a horse-shoe shaped contraption.
suppose	V;PRS;SG;1	suppose	At least, I suppose so.
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	It was vaguely uncomfortable, and appeared sufficiently disturbing that we all volunteered each other in turn until, at last, Andy said he was game.
overcome	V;NFIN	overcome	The hardest part was probably to overcome the gagging reflex that set in when you bit down into the acrid resin jelly and it flowed out and up and around, over teeth, gums and tongue.
fill	V;PST;SG;3	filled	Your nose filled with what you imagined were fumes from the warm paste.
seem	V;PST;2;SG	seemed	And you seemed to be suddenly drowning in your own saliva.
get	V;PST;3;PL	got	Things generally got better when Steven reminded you that you could actually breathe, and swallow, normally.
watch	V;PST;SG;3	watched	He watched the second hand trotting round the dial of his watch mouthing the quarters, before poking the setting goo to check its consistency.
give	V;PST;SG;3	gave	Then, when he gave you the nod, you tried to prise your jaws apart.
think	V;PST;2;SG	thought	As the seal around your gums and teeth was pretty solid, you thought it would never come off, as the others giggled at your expressions and grimaces.
jiggle	V;PST;SG;3	jiggled	Steven jiggled the handle that stuck out beyond your front teeth like some metallic duck’s bill, turning and twisting it most professionally and murmuring encouragements.
suck	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sucking	And, then, with an awful sucking slurp, it came free.
initiate	V;PST;V.PTCP	initiated	The initiated then revelled in retelling the gross sensation of having this evil-smelling plastic slug invade your mouth, while the others waited their turn with mixed trepidation and excitement.
greet	V;PST;SG;3	greeted	Raucous laughter greeted the suggestions of the other body parts that could receive the same treatment.
measure	V;PST;SG;3	measured	Steven measured and mixed the plaster of Paris and prepared for the cast by using a bright blue putty to build up the form of the jaw, and shoring up gaps and holes.
get	V;PRS;3;PL	get	It get even less exciting when he said we’d have to wait an hour while the plaster set real solid.
visit	V;PST;1;PL	visited	But the next time we visited his room we got to marvel at our toothy grins labelled and lined up on the shelf.
want	V;PRS;V.PTCP	wanting	We were all perfectly happy with the result, even wanting one as a trophy until Steven passed an expert eye over them, pointing out here a gap, here a filling, and there a completely misaligned incisor.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	We were, he told us, mediocre specimens for his collection.
matter	V;NFIN	matter	It didn’t matter.
bond	V;PST;V.PTCP	bonded	We were all friends, a group bonded by the common experience of being able to see our teeth on Steven’s shelf.
interview	V;PRS;SG;3	interviews	Wikinews interviews Christopher Hill, U.S. Republican Party presidential candidate
have	V;NFIN	have	What qualifications do you have that will assist you in executing the duties of president?
root	V;PST;V.PTCP	rooted	My Presidential qualifications are rooted in my service as an Officer in the United States Air Force.
take	V;PST;SG;1	took	I took the Oath to our Constitution at 22 years old.
understand	V;PST;SG;1	understood	I understood it was an Oath to ALL of our people.
profess	V;PRS;3;PL	profess	Politicians profess an Oath to this nation but once in office pursue their party agenda and reward their voters.
serve	V;NFIN	serve	As President, I will serve ALL of the people in America, guided by the Constitution.
run	V;NFIN;2	run	Why run for president?
run	V;NFIN;2	run	Why not run for a lower office that you would be more likely to win and effect change?
choose	V;PST;V.PTCP	chosen	When I was a young man in Air Force ROTC I was chosen to attend a unique Pilot Training Program called Euro- NATO Joint Jet Pilot Training.
select	V;PST;SG;3	selected	The Air Force only selected 180 Officers a year from the Academy, Squadron Officer School and ROTC to attend.
volunteer	V;PST;SG;1	volunteered	I volunteered for that program because I knew I wanted to be a Fighter Pilot and at the "Top of the Spear."
graduate	V;PST;2;SG	graduated	If you graduated you were assigned a fighter, if you were not quite fighter pilot material you had no response to fly another aircraft, you were washed out.
prove	V;PST;V.PTCP	proven	Congress has proven itself ineffective as a body.
make	V;NFIN	make	To make a difference in America you must have the "Bully Pulpit" and speak to the People.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The President has that pulpit.
want	V;PST;SG;1	wanted	I never wanted to make politics a career.
choose	V;NFIN	choose	Why did you choose to run in the Republican Party?
date	V;PRS;3;PL	date	My ties to the Republican party date back to 1979, when I volunteered for Ronald Reagan and his campaign in New Hampshire.
believe	V;PST;SG;1	believed	In those days I believed there was a difference in the parties.
understand	V;PRS;SG;1	understand	I understand third party candidates have no success.
hope	V;PRS;SG;1	hope	I hope to lead the Republican party in a new direction for working class Americans.
plan	V;NFIN	plan	How do you plan to get into the Republican debates and make an impact on polls?
invite	V;PST;V.PTCP	invited	In order to be invited to the debates, we must raise money and poll nationally.
list	V;PST;V.PTCP	listed	Recently we were listed in New Hampshire and nationally with Politics1.
receive	V;NFIN	receive	If you do not receive the Republican nomination, would you consider running as a third party candidate?
back	V;NFIN	back	No, I will back the nominee of the Republican party in 2016.
reconsider	V;NFIN	reconsider	After that, I may reconsider.
support	V;PST;V.PTCP	supported	Other than yourself, which candidates have you supported for president in previous campaigns?
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	What are your thoughts on the job Barack Obama has done as president?
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	Specifically, what would you have done differently?
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	President Obama has an agenda that represents the far left wing of his party.
further	V;NFIN	further	His use of the Presidency to further this agenda has in many ways been unconstitutional.
alter	V;PRS;V.PTCP	altering	Specifically, altering law once it is passed to accommodate his voting blocs.
enforce	V;NFIN	enforce	His direction to Eric Holder to enforce some laws and not others is another example.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Hill at the Lesser-known candidates forum in 2011
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	If elected, what would constitute a successful presidency for you?
achieve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Achieving	Achieving four goals:
return	V;PRS;V.PTCP	returning	Term Limits for Congress and the Courts returning government to the People.
implement	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Implementing	Implementing a Tax System that allows working Americans making under $ 250,000 to keep all of their money.
close	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Closing	Closing Loopholes and putting in place a system that is transparent and works.
pass	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Passing	Passing a Living Wage program lifting people out of poverty.
return	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Returning	Returning America to a state of Peace and Security.
like	V;NFIN	like	What individuals would you like to see in a Hill administration?
like	V;NFIN	like	I would like to bring People from the heartland of America that make this nation work.
have	V;PST;V.PTCP	had	We have had too many decades of Ivy League elites and Professors immersed in theory.
like	V;NFIN	like	Anything else you would like to add?
endure	V;NFIN	endure	America is a nation that will endure.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	I believe it is our responsibility to pass on the same opportunities to future generations that were promised to us.
intend	V;PRS;SG;1	intend	As President, I intend to keep that promise!
interview	V;PRS;SG;3	interviews	Wikinews interviews Robert Sarvis, Libertarian Party nominee for Governor of Virginia
answer	V;PST;SG;3	answered	Attorney Robert Sarvis, the Libertarian Party's nominee for Governor of Virginia, answered five questions submitted via e-mail by accredited Wikinews reporter William Saturn.
garner	V;PST;V.PTCP	garnered	Sarvis has garnered double digits in opinion polls for the 2013 Virginia gubernatorial election, an unusual feat for a third party candidate.
place	V;PST;SG;3	placed	The latest Newsmax / Zogby poll from late September placed him at 13 percent, 14 points behind Republican Party nominee Ken Cuccinelli, Virginia's Attorney General, and 19 points behind Democratic Party nominee Terry McAuliffe, the former Democratic National Committee chairman, with 24 percent undecided.
win	V;PRS;SG;3	wins	Whoever wins election on November 5, 2013, would replace outgoing governor Bob McDonnell.
note	V;PRS;SG;3	notes	On his campaign website, Sarvis notes he favors parental school choice through student vouchers, simplification of Virginia's Tax Code, US Second Amendment - gun - rights, same-sex marriage, and reform of the state's drug laws.
discuss	V;PRS;SG;3	discusses	With Wikinews, Sarvis discusses his background, views on McDonnell's tenure, keys to campaign success, plan to implement his agenda, and the former Virginia governor he most admires.
know	V;NFIN	know	What is the most important aspect of your background that voters should know about?
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	I am the only candidate born and raised in Virginia, but also the only candidate who embodies both physically and philosophically the growing diversity of the commonwealth.
mix	V;PST;V.PTCP	mixed	(I am mixed-race (half-Chinese) and in an interracial marriage.)
study	V;PST;V.PTCP	studied	I am the only candidate who has studied both economics and law, and the only candidate with a background in technology entrepreneurship.
understand	V;PRS;SG;1	understand	Because of my academic and professional background, I understand the issues and challenges facing Virginia much better than the other candidates, and I am offering real solutions that can be appreciated across the political spectrum.
believe	V;PRS;SG;3	believes	I am the only candidate who believes in both economic freedom and personal liberty and the only candidate who stands on principle against cronyism and corporatism and in favor of the rule of law.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	What are your thoughts on the job Bob McDonnell has done as Governor of Virginia?
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	What would you have done differently than him?
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	McDonnell did well early in his term pushing back against Senate Democrats who wanted huge tax increases in response to major revenue shortfalls during the recession.
err	V;PST;SG;3	erred	More recently, he erred in accepting a transportation bill that had huge flaws and huge tax increases, rather than prioritizing spending and seeking more rational, efficient, decentralized transportation decision-making.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	He could have shown much greater leadership in pushing for tax reform, regulatory reform, and school choice.
show	V;PST;SG;3	showed	Of course, he showed ethical lapses in judgment.
compare	V;PST;V.PTCP	Compared	Compared to previous third party candidates at the gubernatorial level, you have done exceptionally well in opinion polling.
attribute	V;NFIN	attribute	What do you attribute that success to?
see	V;PRS;3;PL	see	Voters see the two-party system for the sham it has become.
see	V;PRS;3;PL	see	Voters see the two major party nominees as exemplifying what is worst about their respective parties, and they want something better.
meet	V;PRS;3;PL	meet	Voters who meet me and hear me speak know that I am sincere, know that I understand the issues deeply and have workable solutions, and know that I stand on principle against cronyism and corporatism and in favor of the rule of law and the public interest.
like	V;PRS;3;PL	like	People like the fact that I stand for a Virginia that is both Open-minded and Open for Business.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	If elected governor, how will you be able to implement your agenda while dealing with a hostile legislature?
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	There are precedents of independent governors dealing effectively with the legislature, and indeed a wise third-party governor can have greater effectiveness than a major party governor.
share	V;PRS;3;PL	share	Libertarians share many policy positions with each of the major parties.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Working	Working together with both Republicans and Democrats, we can get the good aspects of each party while rejecting the bad parts of both.
elect	V;PRS;V.PTCP	electing	Moreover, electing a third-party governor represents a repudiation of politics as usual, and the major party legislators will face changed constraints and incentives, meaning that much more is possible than many people assume, especially with strong leadership.
admire	V;NFIN	admire	Which previous Virginia Governor(s) do you most admire and why?
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	He was incredibly learned and was a strong advocate for freedom and the rule of law.
highlight	V;PST;V.PTCP	highlighted	Virginia, highlighted in red.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you for your call of this afternoon.
learn	V;NFIN	learn	It was a surprise for us to learn that apparently a decision was reached, perhaps by the judge that the letters are the property of Isabelle.
advise	V;PST;SG;3	advised	Michel advised her on May 25th as follows: "There is a grave legal question involved as to whether or not these letters are the property of your mother's estate and an appropriate legal action is contemplated to determine such question."
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	It is our considered opinion: Williamson should have sent them to the owner's daughter as a very personal matter or auctioned them off when he sold Mrs. Phillips's things on the auction block.
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	He did neither.
sit	V;PST;SG;3	sat	He sat on them 'til some reporter's warned him that they were hot merchandise.
seek	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeking	Then with the idea of seeking cover made a judge (apparently ignorant of the law) appoint an administrator for Mrs. Phillips's estate for stolen merchandise held in posession of acourt appointed guardian of Mrs. Phillips.
cap	V;NFIN	cap	To cap it all he permitted publication of these stolen letters by the press, although they were ordered by him to be under lock and key.
appear	V;PRS;SG;3	appears	Now it appears that Williamson is trying to defend himself to the public.
phone	V;PST;SG;3	phoned	A reporter from the Cleveland Press phoned on Monday last.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	He said that he wanted to check on a statement made by Williamson, since he claimed that while he was Mrs. P's guardian he had never heard from Isabelle.
say	V;PST;1;PL	said	We said; "No comment".
advise	V;PST;V.PTCP	advised	Isabelle was not advised that her mother had a guardian.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	On Dec. 6th 1954 she had a power of attorney duly executed and witnessed by our attorney in Recine, Wis. in favor of Maud Gorham of Marion, Ohio especially to deal in all matters pertaining to Carrie Fulton Phillips.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	We have not seen the Press article of this week, nor one the Marion Star or any Columbus paper might have had, covering an interview with Williamson.
leave	V;NFIN	leave	If he does not leave Isabelle out of all of this he will have to suffer the consequences.
warn	V;NFIN	warn	Perhaps you can warn him for me.
brand	V;NFIN	brand	We will brand him with a libel iron, he won't forget.
cause	V;PST;V.PTCP	caused	The mental and heart suffering caused by this S.O.B to Isabelle is very deep, I can assure you, John.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	If there are no live debts to be satisfied and if the only assets in the reopened estate administrated by Michel are these stolen letters, now apparently determined to be the property of Isabelle, why on that decision alone, cannot these letters be surrendered and the estate be closed because of lack of assets.
succeed	V;NFIN	succeed	In that event, if you can succeed in getting that done, we would like to have the letters surrendered to you for destruction or surrender to the Harding family, as a possible value in checking the accuracy of any published stuff in the future.
insist	V;PRS;SG;3	insists	If the Court insists on these letters being an asset and refuses to close the estate and insists that they be appraised and sold by the administrator at highest value offered, we would want to be sure that the sales price received covers the expenditures and that any trans-action in connection with such a sale remain entirely free from press and publication abuse.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	In our business of antiques, that of antiquarians, we have and must have in certain states, licensed appraisers.
own	V;PRS;3;PL	own	All of them own their own respective businesses and sell.
seem	V;PRS;3;PL	seem	Then of course there seem to be in this picture, so called historians, like the man you mentioned over the phone, Ed. K. Gross etc.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	We even had inquiries from the Wisconsin State College, D. F. Costello, Prof.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	A historian from Minneapolis wrote: would we sell the letters?
want	V;PRS;3;PL	want	These kind of historians want something for nothing and could not appraise anything of this kind, since they do not know corresponding values.
want	V;NFIN	want	But all would want to take a look and see.
want	V;NFIN	want	John, Isabelle does not want to sell the letters unless we are forced to do so by the judge and his court under the auspices of estate jurisdiction.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	She has never seen them, knew nothing of their existence and does not care to see them now or ever.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	There are to ways out: 1) according to Paragraph 1 & 2 of this page and the preceeding paragraph, as we see it.
want	V;NFIN	want	In the first instance we would want to pay you your bill on conclusion or before as you like.
wonder	V;PRS;1;PL	wonder	In the second instance we wonder if you might take for a third of the net or how you would like to be compensated.
intend	V;PRS;SG;3	intends	In one week's time, the Verwoerd Government intends to inaugurate its Republic.
state	V;NFIN	state	It is unnecessary to state that this intention has never been endorsed by the non-white majority of this country.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	The decision has been taken by little over half of the White community; it is opposed by every articulate group amongst the African, Coloured and Indian communities, who constitute the majority of this country.
proceed	V;NFIN	proceed	The Government's intentions to proceed, under these circumstances, has created conditions bordering on crisis.
exclude	V;PST;V.PTCP	excluded	We have been excluded from the Commonwealth, and condemned 95 to 1 at the United Nations.
boycott	V;PST;V.PTCP	boycotted	Our trade is being boycotted, and foreign capital is being withdrawn.
become	V;PRS;V.PTCP	becoming	The country is becoming an armed camp, the Government preparing for civil war with increasingly heavy police and military apparatus, the non-white population for a general strike and long-term non-cooperation with the Government.
draw	V;NFIN	draw	None of us can draw any satisfaction from this developing crisis.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	We, on our part, in the name of the African people - a majority of South Africans - and on the authority given us by 1 400 elected African representatives at the Pietermaritzburg Conference of 25 and 26 March, have put forward serious proposals for a way out of the crisis.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	We have called on the Government to convene an elected National Convention of representatives of all races without delay, and to charge that Convention with the task of drawing up a new Constitution for this country which would be acceptable to all racial groups.
see	V;NFIN	see	We can see no workable alternative to this proposal, except that the Nationalist Government proceeds to enforce a minority decision on all of us, with the certain consequence of still deeper crisis, and a continuing period of strife and disaster ahead.
state	V;PST;V.PTCP	Stated	Stated bluntly, the alternatives appear to be these: talk it out, or shoot it out.
decide	V;PST;V.PTCP	decided	Outside of the Nationalist Party, most of the important and influential bodies of public opinion have clearly decided to talk it out.
welcome	V;PST;V.PTCP	welcomed	The South African Indian Congress, the only substantial Indian community organisation, has welcomed and endorsed the call for a National Convention.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	So, too have the Coloured people, through the Coloured Convention movement which has the backing of the main bodies of Coloured opinion.
represent	V;PST;V.PTCP	represented	A substantial European body of opinion, represented by both the Progressive and the Liberal Parties, has endorsed our call.
come	V;PST;V.PTCP	come	Support for a National Convention has come also from the bulk of the English language press, from several national church organisations, and from many others.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	But where, Sir, does the United Party stand?
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have yet to hear from this most important organisation - the main organisation in fact of anti-Nationalist opinion amongst the European community.
fail	V;PRS;3;PL	fail	If the country's leading statesmen fail to lead at this moment, then the worst is inevitable.
speak	V;NFIN	speak	It is time for you, Sir, and your Party, to speak out.
expect	V;PRS;1;PL	expect	We in South Africa, and the world outside expect an answer.
enable	V;PRS;SG;3	enables	Silence at this time enables, Dr. Verwoerd to lead us onwards towards the brink of disaster.
realise	V;PRS;1;PL	realise	We realise that aspects of our proposal raise complicated problems.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	How shall the representatives be elected?
consult	V;PST;V.PTCP	consulted	Are all groups to be consulted before a constitutional change is made?
delay	V;PST;V.PTCP	delayed	A decision on this matter cannot be delayed.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	Once that decision is taken, then all other matters, of how, when and where, can be discussed, and agreement on them can be reached.
approach	V;PST;V.PTCP	approached	We have approached you and your Party before, and suggested that matters of difference be discussed.
have	V;PST;V.PTCP	had	To date we have had no reply: Nevertheless we still hold the door open.
turn	V;NFIN	turn	It is still not too late to turn the tide against the Nationalist-created crisis.
unite	V;NFIN	unite	It would unite the overwhelming majority of our people, White, Coloured, Indian and African, for a single purpose-round-table talks for a new constitution.
isolate	V;NFIN	isolate	It would isolate the Nationalist Government, and reveal for all time that it is a minority Government, clinging tenaciously to power against the popular will, driving recklessly onward to a disaster for itself and us.
add	V;NFIN	add	Your call for a National Convention now would add such strength to the already powerful call for it that the Government would be chary of ignoring it further.
ignore	V;PRS;3;PL	ignore	And if they nevertheless ignore the call for a Convention, the inter-racial unity thus cemented by your call would lay the basis for the replacement of this Government of national disaster by one more acceptable to the people, one prepared to follow the democratic path of consulting all the people in order to resolve the crisis.
urge	V;PRS;1;PL	urge	We urge you strongly to speak out now.
sign	V;PST;V.PTCP	Signed	[Signed]
celebrate	V;PRS;SG;3	celebrates	NASA celebrates 30th anniversary of first shuttle launch; announces new homes for retired shuttles
announce	V;PRS;SG;3	announces	NASA Administrator Charles Bolden announces where four space shuttle orbiters will be permanently displayed at the conclusion of the Space Shuttle Program during an event commemorating the 30th anniversay of the first shuttle launch on April 12, 2011.
celebrate	V;PST;SG;3	celebrated	NASA celebrated the launch of the first space shuttle Tuesday at an event at the Kennedy Space Center (KSC) in Cape Canaveral, Florida.
lift	V;PST;SG;3	lifted	On April 12, 1981, Space Shuttle Columbia lifted off from the Kennedy Space Center on STS-1, the first space shuttle mission.
hold	V;PST;SG;3	held	NASA held a ceremony commemorating the date outside the hangar, known as Orbiter Processing Facility-1, for Space Shuttle Atlantis, which is being prepped for its final mission which will be STS-135, which will be the last Space Shuttle mission.
announce	V;PST;SG;3	announced	At the ceremony, NASA Administrator Charles Bolden announced the locations that would be given the three remaining Space Shuttle orbiters following the end of the Space Shuttle program.
relocate	V;PST;V.PTCP	relocated	The prototype orbiter, Space Shuttle Enterprise would be relocated from the Smithsonian's National Air and Space Museum Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center at Washington Dulles International Airport to the Intrepid Sea-Air-Space Museum in New York City.
take	V;NFIN	take	Space Shuttle Discovery will take the place of Enterprise at the Udvar-Hazy Center.
retire	V;PST;V.PTCP	retired	Discovery has already been retired following the completion of STS-133 last month, its 39th mission.
undergo	V;PRS;V.PTCP	undergoing	Discovery is undergoing decommissioning and currently being prepped for display by removing toxic materials from the orbiter.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Looking	Looking back: Space Shuttle Columbia lifts off on STS-1 from Launch Pad 39A at the Kennedy Space Center on April 12, 1981.
launch	V;NFIN	launch	Space Shuttle Endeavour, which will launch on STS-134 at the end of the month on April 29, will be sent to the California Science Center in Los Angeles, California following its retirement.
go	V;NFIN	go	Finally, Atlantis will go on display at the Kennedy Space Center Visitor Complex following the orbiter's last flight which launches June 28.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	Columbia was lost tragically back on February 1, 2003 when it disintegrated during re-entry killing all seven astronauts aboard.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	Space Shuttle Challenger was lost when it exploded 73 seconds after liftoff back on January 28, 1986 killing all six aboard.
want	V;PRS;1;PL	want	"We want to thank all of the locations that expressed an interest in one of these national treasures," said Bolden to the gathered crowd which contained many KSC employees.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	"This was a very difficult decision, but one that was made with the American public in mind.
provide	V;PRS;3;PL	provide	In the end, these choices provide the greatest number of people with the best opportunity to share in the history and accomplishments of NASA's remarkable Space Shuttle Program.
choose	V;PST;V.PTCP	chosen	These facilities we've chosen have a noteworthy legacy of preserving space artifacts and providing outstanding access to U.S. and international visitors."
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	This was a very difficult decision, but one that was made with the American public in mind.
provide	V;PRS;3;PL	provide	In the end, these choices provide the greatest number of people with the best opportunity to share in the history and accomplishments of NASA's remarkable Space Shuttle Program.
lift	V;PRS;V.PTCP	lifting	File image of Space Shuttle Atlantis lifting off, which approaches its last mission before retirement.
issue	V;PST;SG;3	issued	U.S. Senator John Cornyn, Republican from Texas, issued a statement regarding the rejection of Johnson Space Center (JSC) in Houston, Texas as a location.
disappoint	V;PST;V.PTCP	disappointed	"Like many Texans, I am disappointed with NASA's decision to slight the Johnson Space Center as a permanent home for one of the Space Shuttle Orbiters.
play	V;PST;V.PTCP	played	Houston has played a critical role throughout the life of the space shuttle, but it is clear political favors trumped common sense and fairness in the selection of the final locations for the orbiter fleet."
add	V;PST;SG;3	added	Cornyn's statement added, "There is no question Houston should have been selected as a final home for one of the orbiters — even Administrator Bolden stated as much.
disappoint	V;PST;V.PTCP	disappointed	Today's announcement is an affront to the thousands of dedicated men and women at Johnson Space Center, the greater Houston community and the State of Texas, and I’m deeply disappointed with the Administration's misguided decision.“
receive	V;NFIN	recieve	However, the JSC will recieve pilot and commander seats from the flight deck.
propose	V;PST;V.PTCP	proposed	The Museum of Flight in Seattle, Washington was also proposed as another location for a shuttle, going so far as to build a new building to house an orbiter.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	In a statement, Governor of Washington Chris Gregoire said, "The Museum of Flight put a tremendous amount of effort into landing a retired shuttle in the Pacific Northwest.
transport	V;PST;V.PTCP	transported	As the home of modern day air travel and the 747, which has gracefully transported shuttles for the last 30 years, Seattle would have been a perfect fit.
disappoint	V;PST;V.PTCP	disappointed	While the Museum of Flight was in the top running, I’m disappointed that NASA did not choose them.
await	V;PRS;3;PL	await	In 2008, Endeavour and Atlantis await a journey on their respective launchpads.
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	"However, the full fuselage trainer, that every astronaut including [former Museum of Flight CEO] Bonnie Dunbar has been trained on, will soon call the Museum of Flight home.
allow	V;NFIN	allow	The largest of the trainers, this addition will allow visitors to actually climb aboard the trainer and experience the hands-on training that astronauts get.
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	Visitors will not be allowed in the other shuttles and this trainer is a true win for our dynamic museum.
help	V;NFIN	help	It will help inspire young people to the adventure of space and to the excitement of a career in science, technology, engineering and math."
disappoint	V;PST;V.PTCP	disappointed	Today's announcement is an affront to the thousands of dedicated men and women at Johnson Space Center, the greater Houston community and the State of Texas, and I’m deeply disappointed with the Administration's misguided decision.
include	V;PRS;3;PL	include	Other items include various shuttle simulators which will be given to the Adler Planetarium in Chicago, Illinois, the Evergreen Aviation & Space Museum of McMinnville, Oregon, and Texas A&M's Aerospace Engineering Department.
offer	V;PRS;V.PTCP	offering	NASA is also offering shuttle heat shield tiles to schools and universities that want to share technology and a piece of space history with their students.
coincide	V;PST;SG;3	coincided	The 30th anniversary of the first shuttle mission coincided with the 50th anniversary of the first manned space flight when Yuri Gagarin lifted off aboard Vostok 1 into space.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	Sensitive Canadian document found on rainy streets
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	A sensitive Environment Canada document was found wet on an Ottawa street by someone who gave it to the CBC today.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	The 131-page document was found on Castlefrank Road in Kanata, Ontario in a rain-stained, tire-marked brown envelope by a passerby.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	The document which had the stamp Protected B on every page detailed various security vulnerabilities of the NEMISIS database which is used by officers to track and prosecute environmental law-breakers.
shut	V;NFIN	shut	If someone were able to shut the system down, or hack inside and delete or corrupt the information, it could affect the prosecution of the lawbreakers.
unveil	V;PST;V.PTCP	unveiled	The database was unveiled in 1999 as the National Enforcement Management Information System and Intelligence System.
refer	V;PST;SG;3	referred	However, on the document, it referred to the database as National Enforcement and Emergency Management Information System and Intelligence System, adding the words "and Emergency" to the name.
classify	V;PST;V.PTCP	classified	Protection B documents are the second lowest grade of classified government documents.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Environment Canada spokesperson Julie Hahn said they apply to letters of complaint, criticism or injury, research or scientific notes and findings that don`t have any conclusion, and Treasury Board letters, agendas and minutes that don`t deal with subjects of national interest.
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	Environment Minister John Baird has asked government officials how such documents that are supposed to be stored in "approved security cabinets" wound up on the street.
tell	V;PST;V.PTCP	told	"I'm told two things: that it's neither classified nor secret and that it's stuff that could be available under access to information," Baird said.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	Environment Canada spokeswoman Sujata Raisinghani told CBC News the department will look into the incident.
take	V;PRS;1;PL	take	"We take document security seriously," she said, but refused to give any more details about how the papers came to be on a road.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Paul Dewar, an Ottawa New Democrat MP, said the combined security lapses raise serious questions about the government's ability to secure sensitive documents.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	Dewar says that bureaucrats of the Conservative government have complained to him about document security to the point that they can`t share the information with their own colleagues.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	However, when a government document has been found on the streets, the government then says it wasn't particularly sensitive.
consume	V;PST;V.PTCP	consumed	"This government is so consumed with control of information and secrecy yet they don't seem to be able to get the fundamentals right," Dewar said.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	In March, a bundle of blueprints for a new headquarters for the military's counterterrorism unit were found stuffed in the trash on a downtown street.
force	V;PST;V.PTCP	forced	In May, Maxime Bernier was forced to resign as Foreign Affairs minister after it became known that he left sensitive documents at the apartment of his then-girlfriend Julie Couillard who had ties to the Hells Angels biker gang.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	"Sensitive government document found on rainy Ottawa street" — CBC.ca, August 15, 2008
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	"Document found on street not secret, say feds" — The Canadian Press, August 15, 2008
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	"Environment Canada security report found on street" — Toronto Star, August 15, 2008
call	V;PRS;SG;3	calls	"MP calls for probe into misplaced documents" — Edmonton Sun, August 16, 2008
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	Published	Published
archive	V;PST;V.PTCP	Archived	Archived
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	As the Western United States burns up with over seventy forest fires in thirteen states and Germany deals with the aftermath of devastating floods, the world's richest man, not counting Vladimir Putin, rocketed himself up to outer space for ten minutes at a cost of two point five million dollars every 60 seconds.
look	V;PST;SG;3	looked	A Democracy Now! Segment on Thursday looked at the irony and dangers of billionaires fighting to privatize and monopolize space infrastructure while the Earth burns due to the man made climate catastrophe.
return	V;PRS;V.PTCP	returning	As an example of his utter lack of self-awareness, Bezos's own comments, after returning from his space adventure, admitted that his wealth is created by not only his customers, but his workers.
remind	V;PRS;SG;3	reminds	This, of course, reminds readers and listeners of the ProPublica story that exposed how little he and the billionaire class pay in taxes.
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	making	They are all the undeserving recipients of corporate socialism, making their space tours.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	looking	I'm looking at you, Richard Branson.
fund	V;PST;V.PTCP	funded	Just another taxpayer funded giveaway to the richest men among us.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	And so I reluctantly thank the goddess of irony.
live	V;PRS;1;PL	live	We live in a country where a living wage for all working men and women is somehow not affordable.
pay	V;PRS;SG;3	pays	But a man who pays almost nothing in taxes is free to go to outer space at the taxpayers' expense.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you.
do	V;PRS;SG;3	does	For what C-Span does.
do	V;PRS;SG;3	does	Mr. McIntosh, my question to you is, how does the space programs by these various millionaire -- billionaires --
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	It shows the failure of trickle down economics upon which you base a lot of your, you know, your stances and everything.
put	V;PRS;V.PTCP	putting	How is -- how is that putting these billions into space instead of improving the conditions of the workers that make these people the richest men on Earth?
know	V;PRS;2;SG	know	You know, I'd like a better understanding on, you know, you're talking about welfare and everything else.
ignore	V;PRS;2;SG	ignore	Yet at the same token, you ignore these stances where these billionaires are pouring money into -- all this money into space.
fly	V;NFIN	fly	The only people who are able to fly with them are also billionaires and millionaires.
force	V;PRS;V.PTCP	forcing	Well, first of all, I'm actually really happy that it's not a government program where they're forcing taxpayers to spend the billions of dollars on something like that.
know	V;PRS;2;SG	know	You know, my view, honestly, is if somebody's been successful in creating a business and making money and the customers have rewarded them, so they've become a billionaire, if they want to spend it on building a rocket to outer space, that's their choice.
want	V;PRS;3;PL	want	If they want to spend it on building mansions, they can do that.
think	V;NFIN	think	I -- I don't think it's the right role of government to tell people how to spend their money.
kid	V;NFIN	kid	David McIntosh, the Club for Growth, can kid himself with his self-assurance, as he did on Washington Journal last Friday, July sixteenth, by believing that the space exploration Bezos partook in was privately funded.
pay	V;PRS;SG;3	pays	But everyone in my neighborhood pays a higher percentage of their income in taxes than he pays on the beyond exorbitant and exceedingly gratuitous wealth that he lives off of.
mention	V;NFIN	mention	Not to mention, The Hill Online newspaper reported in late May that a US competitiveness bill, then being debated on the Senate floor, included ten billion for Bezos's own space exploration company, Blue Origin.
buy	V;PST;V.PTCP	bought	This was also around the time Bezos had bought MGM for nine billion.
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	So we're expected to forklift over, as a simple dinner fork won't cut it, the billions needed for him, again, arguably the richest man on Earth, to acquire more wealth, and as the cherry on top, deliver him one billion more.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to the report published by ProPublica last month, the median American household brings in around seventy thousand a year and pays fourteen percent in federal income taxes.
subsidize	V;PST;3;PL	subsidized	In short, Americans subsidized the fruits of Jeff Bezos' midlife crisis.
worry	V;PRS;SG;1	worry	I worry that we are getting closer and closer to the experience of the little people in the sci-fi film Elysium, starring Matt Damon.
exploit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	exploiting	In the film, the rich, after exploiting the world to the point that it's practically uninhabitable, have moved on to an artificial world, Elysium that might as well be on our moon.
remain	V;PRS;3;PL	remain	And those that remain on Earth, the rest of us, continue working in what could realistically be a future Amazon warehouse.
air	V;PST;SG;3	aired	Later in the Democracy Now! segment, the broadcast aired parts of an MSNBC interview with Bezos, who, in completely out of touch fashion, adamantly expressed that we should preserve our, quote, beautiful gem, end quote, also known as Earth, by transporting polluting industries to outer space, something he acknowledged would take, quote, decades to achieve, end quote.
know	V;PRS;SG;3	knows	Strayer knows much of what he knows because of the experiments he runs at his Applied Cognition Laboratory in Salt Lake City.
measure	V;PRS;SG;3	measures	Often, he measures how people perform one task once they are given a second task.
think	V;PRS;1;PL	think	We think about multitasking as trying to do two separate, cognitively based tasks at the same time.
attempt	V;PRS;SG;3	attempts	A classic example, one that just about everybody attempts to do, is drive a car and talk on a cell phone.
do	V;NFIN	do	We don’t do that well, and more and more people have these gadgets in the car they bring with them, or the car itself becomes a platform for multitasking, and we don’t do it well.
seem	V;NFIN	seem	Now, it would seem obvious — at least to me, maybe I’m wrong — that not all multitasking is either difficult, and certainly not as dangerous, as driving in a car and trying to do something else that’s cognitively and/or physically demanding.
give	V;NFIN	give	Can you give me some examples of what you see as benign or, in fact, productive multitasking?
say	V;NFIN	say	I would actually just say that even the simplest things, like walking and talking, which you’d think would be pretty automatic, turn out to be tasks that compete.
walk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	walking	So, if you’re walking without talking, as soon as you start talking, you’ll see that the pace of your walk changes, and you may be more likely to trip.
depend	V;PRS;V.PTCP	depending	Anything that is depending on attention to be able to process the world is susceptible to dual-task interference, or problems associated with multitasking.
give	V;PRS;1;PL	give	A simple example we give is saying the letters of the alphabet: A, B, C, D, E, or counting, one, two, three, four, five.
try	V;PRS;2;SG	try	But if you try and combine them and do something like, A1, B2, C3, D4, you’ll quickly find that that becomes difficult, you start to forget where you are.
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	And those are two super-well-learned tasks that you can do by themselves.
try	V;PRS;2;SG	try	But when you try and mix them together, you get all jumbled.
start	V;PRS;2;SG	start	As soon as you start to multitask, performance on both of the tasks start to degrade.
attempt	V;PRS;V.PTCP	attempting	Now, how much of that is a function of attempting to multitask versus the fact that we learn habits and patterns and memorize things that become rote, that we don’t need to engage our cognitive process at all?
become	V;NFIN	become	You can become automatic at certain aspects of performance.
happen	V;PRS;3;PL	happen	So, a lot of things kind of just happen without consciously thinking about them.
demand	V;PRS;3;PL	demand	But new things, new activities or new environments, demand you to pay attention, to allocate the prefrontal cortex to process some of that information that’s new and novel.
stress	V;PRS;V.PTCP	stressing	That’s the real problem, is that if you’re always in a situation where everything’s exactly routine, you’re not stressing the parts of the brain that are responsible for multitasking.
have	V;PRS;2;SG	have	But as soon as you have some novel activity — like talking, like driving a car, riding a bicycle — you have to pay attention to what you’re doing.
have	V;PRS;2;SG	have	You have to engage the prefrontal executive attentional networks.
know	V;NFIN	know	Do you know anything, or think much about, multitasking and multitasking failures in other high-stakes settings beyond driving, whether it’s military decisions or political decisions, business decisions?
look	V;PST;V.PTCP	looked	So we’ve looked in the medical domain, medical human factors: you can see it in terms of, say, delivery of medicine at a pharmacy.
interrupt	V;PST;V.PTCP	interrupted	If the pharmacist is constantly being interrupted with calls, they’re going to fill the prescription incorrectly.
distract	V;PST;V.PTCP	distracted	If an anesthesiologist or a surgeon is distracted by the technology in the operating room, that can create a problem.
have	V;PRS;2;SG	have	In the operating room, it’s not just the phone, although that’s sometimes present, but you have all the displays and technology.
create	V;PRS;V.PTCP	creating	Each one of those are creating alarms that creates this whirl of noise, and alerts and distractions that compete for the anesthesiologist’s and the surgeon’s attention.
read	V;PRS;V.PTCP	reading	So I’ve been reading about how the modern worker, let’s say in the U.S. — they could be in marketing, they could be at a nonprofit, could be in finance, whatever — that if you’re working with a computer, which describes many, many, many, many of us now obviously, that one big change in the last 15 or 20 years is how many pieces of software and how many platforms are — I don’t want to say fighting for your attention necessarily, that sounds a little a little more pejorative than I mean it to be, but how in the course of your workday, you need to switch and switch and switch and switch.
have	V;NFIN	have	We all may have multiple pieces of communication software, multiple pieces of productivity software, and so on.
talk	V;NFIN	talk	Can you talk about that, what seems to be a relatively low-stakes environment — the workplace in a marketing firm or whatever — but how our attention is being carved up during the course of the day?
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, the research shows that you have a loss of productivity when you’re trying to multitask — just the opposite of what you think.
think	V;NFIN	think	So you may think “If I’m multitasking, I’m going to get a lot more things done.”
happen	V;PRS;SG;3	happens	But what happens is, a big chunk of our day is lost as we’re switching from one task to the other to the other.
find	V;PRS;3;PL	find	What they find is that the best way to be productive in the office is to try and focus on one task at a time, focus on just sending that memo out, writing that email, doing whatever that operation is for a short period of time, then take a break and then come back to it.
try	V;IMP;2	try	Don’t try and juggle back and forth because you’ll get confused, you’ll get lost, you’ll be this — “Where was I?” kind of switch cost.
confirm	V;PST;V.PTCP	confirmed	This year has once again confirmed the importance of the United Nations as the truly indispensable multilateral forum for addressing today’s global problems.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	The humanitarian crisis in the Horn of Africa, ecological disasters such as Fukushima, political developments like the Arab Spring and terrorist attacks such as the assault on the United Nations building in Abuja last August require concerted action by the United Nations and its Member States.
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	Ten years after 9/11, joint action in the fight against terrorism continues to remain central on the multilateral agenda.
witness	V;PST;V.PTCP	witnessed	In the past nine months, we have witnessed momentous changes in the Arab world that nobody expected when we met one year ago.
drive	V;PST;V.PTCP	driven	Pressure for change is driven by the wish of women and men to choose their own fate and to improve their chances for a better life.
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	An overwhelming number of young people were at the forefront of this enormous tide, which started in Tunisia and Egypt.
pay	V;NFIN	pay	We should pay tribute to their courage.
lose	V;PST;3;PL	lost	In Libya, thousands lost their lives in the struggle for freedom and democracy.
condemn	V;PRS;SG;3	condemns	Austria strongly condemns the systematic human rights violations and the violence against peaceful demonstrators.
urge	V;PRS;1;PL	urge	We strongly urge those responsible to immediately stop the bloodshed and to engage in meaningful dialogue and reforms.
support	V;PRS;SG;3	supports	Austria supports the Libyan people in its struggle for freedom.
block	V;PST;V.PTCP	blocked	Over € 150 million blocked in Austrian bank accounts has been de-frozen for humanitarian use.
provide	V;PST;V.PTCP	provided	Both the Austrian Government and private sector have provided medicine, relief supplies and desperately needed fuel to Libya.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	The international community and the United Nations have to support and assist the transition process in the Arab world as the United Nations does now in Libya.
last	V;NFIN	last	This period of change may last for some time and will be full of challenges.
welcome	V;PRS;SG;3	welcomes	Austria welcomes all efforts undertaken by Governments in the region for peaceful and credible change.
encourage	V;PRS;1;PL	encourage	We encourage the newly empowered authorities to create a constitutional framework based on democracy and human rights, in order to fulfil their mandate for democratic change responsibly and peacefully.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	The events in the Arab world notwithstanding, we must continue to focus on the Middle East peace process.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	The Middle East took centre stage this week here at the General Assembly — and rightly so.
build	V;NFIN	build	We must build trust and we have to foster the belief among Israelis and Palestinians that a negotiated settlement can be achieved — a sustainable solution based on two States living side by side in a secure and peaceful neighbourhood within mutually recognized borders.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have no choice but to return to direct negotiations between the two parties.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	The Quartet, in its statement yesterday, has shown a way to do that and has also proposed concrete timelines.
support	V;PRS;SG;3	supports	Austria fully supports the Quartet statement.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is no time to lose.
welcome	V;PRS;SG;3	welcomes	Austria welcomes President Al-Nasser’s choice of theme for this year’s general debate.
appreciate	V;PRS;1;PL	appreciate	We appreciate Qatar’s strong track record in that regard.
remind	V;PST;SG;3	reminded	Last year’s tenth anniversary of the adoption of Security Council resolution 1325 (2000) on women, peace and security reminded us that the promise of women’s full and equal participation in all efforts of maintaining peace and security, including in mediation processes, has not yet come true and that we all need to intensify our efforts.
let	V;IMP;2	let	In that context, let me also mention an Austrian contribution to international mediation efforts.
invite	V;PRS;V.PTCP	inviting	By inviting political decision-makers from both Khartoum and Juba to Vienna in recent years, Austria has managed to offer the two sides a platform for discussion.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	We are ready to continue to do so.
have	V;PRS;3;PL	have	The Sudan and South Sudan have a shared past and must resolve their remaining problems peacefully.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	The United Nations headquarters in Vienna serves as a dynamic hub for the promotion of peace, security and sustainable development.
announce	V;NFIN	announce	I am proud to announce that a liaison office of the United Nations Office for Disarmament Affairs will open in Vienna soon.
set	V;PST;SG;3	set	Over the past year, the International Anti-Corruption Academy set up its headquarters near Vienna, and the Vienna Centre for Disarmament and Non-Proliferation and an office of the International Peace Institute were established.
stand	V;PRS;SG;3	stands	Austria fully stands behind the campaign launched by the Secretary-General on Monday to achieve universal access to modern energy services.
support	V;PRS;1;PL	support	We support doubling the rate of improvement in energy efficiency and the share of renewable energy in the global energy mix by 2030.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	Austria provides substantial support to the energy and environment efforts undertaken by the United Nations Industrial Development Organization.
host	V;PST;1;PL	hosted	Last June, we hosted the Vienna Energy Forum, where about 1,400 participants discussed alternative energy concepts to reach the energy goals of the United Nations.
become	V;NFIN	become	The Fukushima nuclear catastrophe should become a turning point in our approach to nuclear safety.
owe	V;PRS;1;PL	owe	We owe it to future generations to make safety concerns come first when using nuclear power.
decide	V;PST;SG;3	decided	Austria decided, in 1978, to forgo the use of nuclear energy.
adopt	V;PST;V.PTCP	adopted	Some countries have recently adopted a similar course, which we welcome.
wish	V;PRS;SG;1	wish	I wish to make a ministerial statement about the first confirmed case of COVID-19 in New Zealand.
record	V;PST;SG;3	recorded	On Friday, 28 February, New Zealand recorded its first case of the COVID-19 coronavirus, becoming the 48th country to have a confirmed case.
enter	V;PST;V.PTCP	entered	The individual had entered the country two days earlier after spending some weeks in Iran, which is currently experiencing a sizable outbreak of the disease.
report	V;NFIN	report	I can report to the House that they are currently in a stable and improving condition at Auckland Hospital.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	The good news in this instance is that the individual had limited contact with people upon their return to the country, and their family took all the appropriate measures when presenting at the emergency department.
expect	V;NFIN	expect	As you would expect, our public health service is working hard to contain this first case and limit the risk of any spread of the disease.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	Contact tracing began immediately and has gone to plan.
seat	V;PST;V.PTCP	seated	All 15 people who were seated nearby the individual on their flight into New Zealand have been traced and are being told to self-isolate.
remain	V;NFIN	remain	Public health staff will remain in touch with individuals to monitor the situation and give ongoing advice.
remain	V;PRS;V.PTCP	remaining	All but 10 of the remaining passengers on the flight, who are not considered to be close contacts, have also been contacted to provide reassurance and answer any questions they might have; those 10 that haven't been contacted did not provide accurate contact information.
advise	V;PST;V.PTCP	advised	I'm advised that as a result of our decisive response, the chance of an outbreak from our first case is low.
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	Internationally, the situation with COVID-19 continues to develop at pace.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	However, the Director-General of the World Health Organization said, this morning, containment … is feasible and must remain the top priority for all countries.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	taking	This is exactly the approach that New Zealand is taking, using three main tools: border controls; self-isolation; and good public health practices, including highlighting the importance of basic hygiene.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	At every step, the Government has taken decisions in the interests of health and safety of the wider public, and so far we have been among the best at keeping COVID-19 at bay.
decide	V;PST;SG;3	decided	Just yesterday, Cabinet decided to extend for another week the travel restrictions that have been in place for China since 3 February and for Iran since last Friday.
play	V;PST;V.PTCP	played	These restrictions have played a key role in keeping COVID-19 out of New Zealand for so long and have given us time to better understand the disease and ramp up our preparations.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	Of course, members will be aware that there are outbreaks in other regions as well.
decide	V;PST;V.PTCP	decided	So, as a further precaution, we have decided that anyone arriving in New Zealand who has been in northern Italy or South Korea who has been there in the last 14 days will be registered for self-isolation.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	This is based on an expert assessment of risk.
recognize	V;PRS;SG;3	recognises	It recognises that while there are outbreaks in both countries, they also have well-developed health systems and the outbreaks are largely located in specific regions.
know	V;PRS;1;PL	know	We know self-isolation works.
manage	V;PRS;V.PTCP	managing	It is a longstanding and successful approach to managing infectious diseases and has proven its worth again in recent weeks.
step	V;PST;V.PTCP	stepped	We have also stepped up the health presence at our airports.
fly	V;PRS;V.PTCP	flying	All airlines flying into New Zealand have been requested to read a health message upon landing, and all arrivals into New Zealand will receive health information cards alongside the standard customs declaration form.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	As a result of decisions taken on Friday, health staff are meeting all direct international flights landing in New Zealand airports from Hong Kong, Japan, South Korea, Singapore, and Thailand.
ramp	V;PRS;V.PTCP	ramping	Our border response is further ramping up to have health staff available for all international flights into New Zealand.
design	V;PST;V.PTCP	designed	All these decisions and actions are designed to limit the spread of any sporadic COVID-19 cases that might arrive in New Zealand.
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	At the same time, our health system continues its preparations in line with our comprehensive pandemic plan.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	I want to thank our DHBs, public health staff, and primary care and health officials for the work they are doing to protect the public.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	I have high confidence in our medical professionals.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have a strong public health system.
continue	V;PRS;1;PL	continue	We continue to work to keep COVID-19 out, but we are ready if it arrives in our community.
state	V;NFIN;2	State	State the postulates of Dalton’s atomic theory
use	V;NFIN;2	Use	Use postulates of Dalton’s atomic theory to explain the laws of definite and multiple proportions
record	V;PST;V.PTCP	recorded	The earliest recorded discussion of the basic structure of matter comes from ancient Greek philosophers, the scientists of their day.
argue	V;PST;3;PL	argued	In the fifth century BC, Leucippus and Democritus argued that all matter was composed of small, finite particles that they called atomos, a term derived from the Greek word for “indivisible.”
think	V;PST;3;PL	thought	They thought of atoms as moving particles that differed in shape and size, and which could join together.
come	V;PST;3;PL	came	Later, Aristotle and others came to the conclusion that matter consisted of various combinations of the four “elements” —fire, earth, air, and water—and could be infinitely divided.
think	V;PST;3;PL	thought	Interestingly, these philosophers thought about atoms and “elements” as philosophical concepts, but apparently never considered performing experiments to test their ideas.
hold	V;PST;SG;3	held	The Aristotelian view of the composition of matter held sway for over two thousand years, until English schoolteacher John Dalton helped to revolutionize chemistry with his hypothesis that the behavior of matter could be explained using an atomic theory.
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	First published in 1807, many of Dalton’s hypotheses about the microscopic features of matter are still valid in modern atomic theory.
compose	V;PST;V.PTCP	composed	1. Matter is composed of exceedingly small particles called atoms.
participate	V;NFIN	participate	An atom is the smallest unit of an element that can participate in a chemical change.
consist	V;PRS;SG;3	consists	2. An element consists of only one type of atom, which has a mass that is characteristic of the element and is the same for all atoms of that element (Figure 2.2).
contain	V;PRS;SG;3	contains	A macroscopic sample of an element contains an incredibly large number of atoms, all of which have identical chemical properties.
contain	V;PRS;SG;3	contains	A pre-1982 copper penny (left) contains approximately 3 ×× 1022 copper atoms (several dozen are represented as brown spheres at the right), each of which has the same chemical properties.
differ	V;PRS;3;PL	differ	3. Atoms of one element differ in properties from atoms of all other elements.
consist	V;PRS;SG;3	consists	4. A compound consists of atoms of two or more elements combined in a small, whole-number ratio.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	In a given compound, the numbers of atoms of each of its elements are always present in the same ratio (Figure 2.3).
result	V;PRS;SG;3	results	Copper(II) oxide, a powdery, black compound, results from the combination of two types of atoms—copper (brown spheres) and oxygen (red spheres)—in a 1:1 ratio.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	5. Atoms are neither created nor destroyed during a chemical change, but are instead rearranged to yield substances that are different from those present before the change (Figure 2.4).
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	When the elements copper (a shiny, red-brown solid, shown here as brown spheres) and oxygen (a clear and colorless gas, shown here as red spheres) react, their atoms rearrange to form a compound containing copper and oxygen (a powdery, black solid).
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	Dalton’s atomic theory provides a microscopic explanation of the many macroscopic properties of matter that you’ve learned about.
consist	V;PRS;SG;3	consists	For example, if an element such as copper consists of only one kind of atom, then it cannot be broken down into simpler substances, that is, into substances composed of fewer types of atoms.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	And if atoms are neither created nor destroyed during a chemical change, then the total mass of matter present when matter changes from one type to another will remain constant (the law of conservation of matter).
test	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Testing	Testing Dalton’s Atomic Theory
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	In the following drawing, the green spheres represent atoms of a certain element.
represent	V;PRS;3;PL	represent	The purple spheres represent atoms of another element.
touch	V;PRS;3;PL	touch	If the spheres touch, they are part of a single unit of a compound.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	Does the following chemical change represented by these symbols violate any of the ideas of Dalton’s atomic theory?
consist	V;PRS;3;PL	consist	The starting materials consist of two green spheres and two purple spheres.
consist	V;PRS;3;PL	consist	The products consist of only one green sphere and one purple sphere.
violate	V;PRS;SG;3	violates	This violates Dalton’s postulate that atoms are neither created nor destroyed during a chemical change, but are merely redistributed.
appear	V;PRS;3;PL	appear	(In this case, atoms appear to have been destroyed.)
check	V;IMP;2	Check	Check Your Learning
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	In the following drawing, the green spheres represent atoms of a certain element.
represent	V;PRS;3;PL	represent	The purple spheres represent atoms of another element.
touch	V;PRS;3;PL	touch	If the spheres touch, they are part of a single unit of a compound.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	Does the following chemical change represented by these symbols violate any of the ideas of Dalton’s atomic theory?
consist	V;PRS;3;PL	consist	The starting materials consist of four green spheres and two purple spheres.
consist	V;PRS;3;PL	consist	The products consist of four green spheres and two purple spheres.
violate	V;NFIN	violate	This does not violate any of Dalton’s postulates:
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	Atoms are neither created nor destroyed, but are redistributed in small, whole-number ratios.
describe	V;NFIN;2	Describe	Describe the reasons why many Americans doubted that Abraham Lincoln would be reelected
explain	V;NFIN;2	Explain	Explain how the Union forces overpowered the Confederacy
describe	V;NFIN;2	Describe	Describe the contributions and experiences of African Americans serving in the Civil War
mobilize	V;PST;V.PTCP	mobilized	By the outset of 1864, after three years of war, the Union had mobilized its resources for the ongoing struggle on a massive scale.
oversee	V;PST;V.PTCP	overseen	The government had overseen the construction of new railroad lines and for the first time used standardized rail tracks that allowed the North to move men and materials with greater ease.
shift	V;PST;V.PTCP	shifted	The North’s economy had shifted to a wartime model.
mobilize	V;PST;SG;3	mobilized	The Confederacy also mobilized, perhaps to a greater degree than the Union, its efforts to secure independence and maintain slavery.
experience	V;PST;SG;3	experienced	Yet the Confederacy experienced ever - greater hardships after years of war.
face	V;PST;SG;3	faced	Without the population of the North, it faced a shortage of manpower.
compare	V;PST;V.PTCP	compared	The lack of industry, compared to the North, undercut the ability to sustain and wage war.
lower	V;PST;3;PL	lowered	Rampant inflation as well as food shortages in the South lowered morale.
place	V;PST;SG;3	placed	From the beginning of the war, the Confederacy placed great hope in being recognized and supported by Great Britain and France.
remain	V;PST;SG;3	remained	European intervention in the conflict remained a strong possibility, but when it did occur, it was not in a way anticipated by either the Confederacy or the Union.
believe	V;PST;SG;3	believed	Napoleon III of France believed the Civil War presented an opportunity for him to restore a French empire in the Americas.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	With the United States preoccupied, the time seemed ripe for action.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	Napoleon’s target was Mexico, and in 1861, a large French fleet took Veracruz.
move	V;PST;3;PL	moved	The French then moved to capture Mexico City, but the advance came to an end when Mexican forces defeated the French in 1862.
conquer	V;NFIN	conquer	Despite this setback, France eventually did conquer Mexico, establishing a regime that lasted until 1867.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	Rather than coming to the aid of the Confederacy, France used the Civil War to provide a pretext for efforts to reestablish its former eighteenth - century colonial holdings.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Still, the Confederacy had great confidence that it would find an ally in Great Britain despite the antislavery sentiment there.
hope	V;PST;3;PL	hoped	Southerners hoped Britain’s dependence on cotton for its textile mills would keep the country on their side.
prove	V;PST;3;PL	proved	The fact that the British proved willing to build and sell ironclad ships intended to smash through the Union naval blockade further raised Southern hopes.
purchase	V;PST;SG;3	purchased	The Confederacy purchased two of these armored blockade runners, the CSS Florida and the CSS Alabama.
destroy	V;PST;V.PTCP	destroyed	Both were destroyed during the war.
work	V;PST;SG;3	worked	The Confederacy’s staunch commitment to slavery eventually worked against British recognition and support, since Great Britain had abolished slavery in 1833.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	The 1863 Emancipation Proclamation ended any doubts the British had about the goals of the Union cause.
cheer	V;PST;SG;3	cheered	In the aftermath of the proclamation, many in Great Britain cheered for a Union victory.
disappoint	V;PST;SG;3	disappointed	Ultimately, Great Britain, like France, disappointed the Confederacy’s hope of an alliance, leaving the outnumbered and out - resourced states that had left the Union to fend for themselves.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	At the beginning of the war, in 1861 and 1862, Union forces had used contrabands, or escaped enslaved people, for manual labor.
lead	V;PST;SG;3	led	The Emancipation Proclamation, however, led to the enrollment of African American men as Union soldiers.
enslave	V;PST;V.PTCP	enslaved	Huge numbers of formerly enslaved as well as free Black people from the North enlisted, and by the end of the war in 1865, their numbers had swelled to over 190,000.
run	V;PST;SG;3	ran	Racism among White people in the Union army ran deep, however, fueling the belief that Black soldiers could never be effective or trustworthy.
fear	V;PST;SG;3	feared	The Union also feared for the fate of captured Black soldiers.
see	V;PST;3;PL	saw	Although many Black soldiers saw combat duty, these factors affected the types of tasks assigned to them.
limit	V;PST;V.PTCP	limited	Many Black regiments were limited to hauling supplies, serving as cooks, digging trenches, and doing other types of labor, rather than serving on the battlefield (Figure 15.16).
illustrate	V;PRS;SG;3	illustrates	This 1865 daguerreotype illustrates three of the Union’s distinct advantages: African American soldiers, a stream of cannons and supplies, and an extensive railroad grid. (credit: Library of Congress)
receive	V;PST;3;PL	received	African American soldiers also received lower wages than their White counterparts: ten dollars per month, with three dollars deducted for clothing.
receive	V;PST;3;PL	received	White soldiers, in contrast, received thirteen dollars monthly, with no deductions.
work	V;PST;3;PL	worked	Abolitionists and their Republican supporters in Congress worked to correct this discriminatory practice, and in 1864, Black soldiers began to receive the same pay as White soldiers plus retroactive pay to 1863 (Figure 15.17).
pose	V;PRS;3;PL	pose	African American and White soldiers of the Union army pose together in this photograph, although in reality, Black soldiers were often kept separate and given only menial jobs.
welcome	V;PST;3;PL	welcomed	For their part, African American soldiers welcomed the opportunity to prove themselves.
enslave	V;PST;V.PTCP	enslaved	Some 85 percent were formerly enslaved people who were fighting for the liberation of all of the enslaved and the end of slavery.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	When given the opportunity to serve, many Black regiments did so heroically.
distinguish	V;PST;SG;3	distinguished	One such regiment, the Fifty-Fourth Regiment of Massachusetts Volunteers, distinguished itself at Fort Wagner in South Carolina by fighting valiantly against an entrenched Confederate position.
give	V;PST;3;PL	gave	They willingly gave their lives for the cause.
show	V;PST;SG;3	showed	The Confederacy, not surprisingly, showed no mercy to African American troops.
attempt	V;PST;3;PL	attempted	In April 1864, Southern forces attempted to take Fort Pillow in Tennessee from the Union forces that had captured it in 1862.
overrun	V;PST;3;PL	overran	Confederate troops under Major General Nathan Bedford Forrest, the future founder of the Ku Klux Klan, quickly overran the fort, and the Union defenders surrendered.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	taking	Instead of taking the African American soldiers prisoner, as they did the White soldiers, the Confederates executed them.
outrage	V;PST;SG;3	outraged	The massacre outraged the North, and the Union refused to engage in any future exchanges of prisoners with the Confederacy.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	So in today's video, I'm finally gonna be doing a room tour.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	I know this is something that you guys have requested for a very long time, and after two and a half years of living here, I finally feel like I've made it my own and decorated it how I want, and I'm ready to show it to you guys.
let	V;IMP;2	let	Alright so let's start off with the bed and the couch area.
redo	V;PRS;V.PTCP	redoing	Honestly, if I was redoing this from the beginning, I would have really liked to have a double bed in this room, but, unfortunately, with the space as you can probably see from that wall there till the door, it's basically just enough to fit a bed and a couch, and this is my couch over here.
change	V;PST;V.PTCP	changed	I've recently changed it, and if you've been following my channel for a long time, then you'll know I'm really proud of how I color match the pictures on the wall over here with sort of the gray and yellow vibe, the gray and yellow cushions.
fit	V;PRS;SG;3	fits	This couch is also quite big, it fits three people quite comfortably, and if I have family or friends staying over, it opens up into a full double bed.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	This coffee table over here, it's glass and has two levels, so it's really good when we have multiple friends over, and we have lots of food to put on the table, or if we're playing board games, we can have people sit around it, and still have a lot of space to lay everything out.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	So there's nothing too special about the bed itself, but it does have a very important function, which, it has a lot of storage.
keep	V;PRS;SG;1	keep	So down here is basically where I keep everything that I can't really keep in my room, because there's not enough space.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	I've got an ironing board, all of my suitcases, socks, clothes that I don't wear that often, anything that I need for fixing the house like screwdrivers, drills, nails, all these things that I definitely wouldn't want to have laid out in my room, but I still need somewhere in the house.
love	V;PRS;SG;1	love	I love that this room has floor-to-ceiling windows as well, it really lets the light come in, especially in the summer time.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	I know London doesn't have that much light, but I'll take as much as I can, and there's also a small balcony out here, which is fairly rare for London.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have two chairs where we can sit down and sort of enjoy the weather when it is a little bit better, um but now it's really cold so just gonna close it.
see	V;NFIN	see	And then a little behind the scenes that you probably will never see again in a video is that down here I keep all of the filming gear that doesn't really fit anywhere else in the room: two sets of tripods, I also have a c-stand.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	Okay next to my bed, I've got the radio alarm clock, and this has actually changed my life.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	It makes waking up in the morning and getting out of bed at 6:00 a.m. when it's pitch black outside so much easier when you're waking up really early.
wake	V;NFIN	wake	It's so much better to wake up to the sound of somebody talking to you: your favorite radio station, some music, whatever, instead of that classic iPhone alarm, "ba, ba, ba."
buy	V;PST;SG;1	bought	Over here on the wall, these are posters that I actually bought back in 2013 in my first year of university in Toronto, and I really really liked them so I've kept them around.
manage	V;PST;SG;1	managed	Also I'm really proud that I managed to put them up all by myself, they look quite neat, and I feel like they're equally spaced, so I'm really proud of how they turned out.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	Today I'm doing another college day in my life.
leave	V;PST;SG;1	left	And I just left Starbucks, don't mind what I look like.
wake	V;PST;SG;1	woke	I literally just pretty much woke up and left this morning.
start	V;PRS;V.PTCP	starting	I'm starting the review for all the chapters for my final exam, and it is a lot of information so I already planned on getting up early today and going.
