end	V;PST;SG;1	ended	So I ended up having to order them, but she said there was no way to do a direct exchange.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	So she had to give me a return and then re-ring it up.
do	V;NFIN	do	And oh my gosh I was literally in there for like 25 minutes all to just do a simple exchange for the exact same shoe.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	But it's over now and they're gonna send them to me in the mail.
have	V;NFIN	have	I'm just kind of sad that they didn't have them in stock, so now I have to wait.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	But oh well it's over with now, we're gonna go get lunch.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	Some of the distinctive skyline of Downtown, including the Kaiser Building and the Cathedral of Christ the Light.
overshadow	V;PST;V.PTCP	overshadowed	Often overshadowed by San Francisco, its larger neighbor lying directly to the west, Oakland has in recent years began to step out from under the shadows, playing upon the beautiful highlights of its own and its rise as the multicultural "hipster' city of the Bay Area.
nurture	V;PST;V.PTCP	nurtured	Oakland's history in the arts and entertainment arena is notable as well, as Oakland has nurtured or been a second home to novelists Gertrude Stein, Jack London, Amy Tan, and Maya Angelou; actors Meryl Streep, Mark Hamill, Bruce Lee, and Tom Hanks; architect Julia "Butterfly" Hill; classical conductor Calvin Simmons; rappers MC Hammer, 2Pac, Messy Marv, and several other notables in the liberal arts and sciences.
reflect	V;PRS;SG;3	reflects	Oakland as a city reflects the amazing diversity of its residents and long history.
visit	V;NFIN	visit	For many visitors, the first place they are likely to visit is Downtown, especially if they are in town on business, or simply do not know where to start.
want	V;PRS;SG;3	wants	The center of transportation by BART and by bus is there, if one wants to branch out.
walk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	walking	And it is as simple as walking in a direction from there, for the first few neighborhoods, since Old Oakland is along 10th Street, just west of Broadway.
continue	V;PRS;V.PTCP	continuing	Just east of Broadway and continuing north and south is Oakland's famous Chinatown, and that to get the real essence of "Chinatown," Oakland rather than San Francisco is your best bet.
reflect	V;PRS;3;PL	reflect	Many of the buildings and streets of Chinatown reflect the diverse nature of its history, in architecture and in the bilingual signs seen throughout the neighborhood.
venture	V;NFIN	venture	The curious traveler can venture to take a bus southwards along Broadway, and come to Jack London Square.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	The unlikely mixture of warehouses and very expensive restaurants and posh atmosphere alongside some of the landmarks of the city's waterfront makes the Square an interesting place to visit and explore, even for locals.
preserve	V;PST;V.PTCP	preserved	It's the home of the USS Potomac and the site of the namesake author's residence, still preserved and humbly standing not far from a saloon from the same era.
buzz	V;PRS;SG;3	buzzes	In the evening it buzzes with art galleries, theaters and residents and Bay Area visitors coming into the area to see concerts.
mix	V;PST;V.PTCP	mixed	A popular area on the weekend with a mixed vibe of hipster and hip hop.
range	V;PRS;V.PTCP	ranging	It's a burgeoning neighborhood of performing arts ranging from nightclubs to music halls, avant-garde performance art, and even an improv theater company.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Uptown also has some of the best in the city's vintage architecture, like the Gothic Revival Cathedral Building and the Art Deco Fox and Paramount Theaters, as well as several restaurants.
refer	V;PRS;V.PTCP	referring	North Oakland is a sweeping term, referring to just about everywhere north of Uptown and Lake Merritt.
seek	V;PRS;3;PL	seek	Many of the neighborhoods are commercial centers and absolute heaven for foodies or those who seek quieter surroundings than the hustle and bustle of Downtown and Chinatown.
lie	V;PRS;SG;3	lies	Temescal is a neighborhood that lies north-northwest from Downtown, centered along the reach of Telegraph Avenue, at and near 51st Street.
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	Until the 1960s, a dominantly Italian-American neighborhood, it is now a much more diversified neighborhood, home to many restaurants, including some of the city's best Korean and Ethiopian establishments.
lie	V;PRS;SG;3	lies	Northeast of Temescal and near the Berkeley border lies Rockridge, a neighborhood mixed with family friendly and artistic vibes.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	It has lots of local boutiques and a diverse range of food at all prices and styles.
make	V;PRS;3;PL	make	Open-air markets, bookstores, and a BART station all its own make Rockridge another of many pleasant stops in North Oakland.
lie	V;PRS;SG;3	lies	South of Rockridge, and southeast of Temescal lies Piedmont Avenue, a street extending from Broadway near 34th and Hawthorne and an area with a group of hospitals on a hill (Pill Hill) up and through a neighborhood bordered on both sides by picturesque hills, houses and trees.
rate	V;PST;V.PTCP	rated	Another of North Oakland's group of foodie spots, home to the city's single Michelin-rated restaurant and many others; cafes, ice cream, coffee shops and pleasant walks complete the vision of this interesting and quiet neighborhood.
separate	V;PST;V.PTCP	separated	East along I-580 and not far from Piedmont Avenue are two neighborhoods separated by a couple of blocks.
share	V;PRS;3;PL	share	Grand Lake and Lakeshore both share a friendly, suburban sort of feel.
stand	V;PRS;SG;3	stands	The Grand Lake Theater stands at the entrance to the neighborhood that bears its name, a matter of a few hundred feet from Lakeshore Avenue – both are diverse neighborhoods no matter what your budget, be it burgers or sandwiches or a full romantic evening, either of these twin-sister neighborhoods will make a pleasant time, by day or night.
burgeon	V;PRS;V.PTCP	burgeoning	West Oakland was once a burgeoning working-class neighborhood with its roots tracing as far back as the opening decades of the 19th century.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	It has seen a period of decline between the 1960s and the more recent past.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	Strangely, twice in its history the neighborhood has seen benefits from major earthquakes.
relocate	V;PST;3;PL	relocated	After the 1906 earthquake, many San Franciscans relocated there, and the industrial development of the 20s and 30s saw its population in the hundreds of thousands.
destroy	V;PST;V.PTCP	destroyed	With Loma Prieta in 1989, the effective barrier of the Cypress Street Overpass was destroyed, and the decades since have seen the rebuilding and refurbishing of many of the neighborhood's original Victorian houses, and there is a burgeoning performing arts community growing near the center of the neighborhood.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	Though there are a few draws for the traveler, West Oakland is dominantly a very poor working-class residential neighborhood with a less than spectacular reputation.
raise	V;PST;V.PTCP	raised	They are either raised coral limestone or coral atolls.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	The beautiful harbour opposite the main town of Neiafu (known as the Port of Refuge Harbour) is a common destination for yachties sailing the South Pacific.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	The waters of the islands are known for their clarity, it being said that you can see the bottom at 40 metres.
attract	V;PRS;SG;3	attracts	The area attracts many humpback whales between June and November and there are lots of companies organising tours to see them.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	There are numerous places to stay to suit most budgets.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
suggest	V;PRS;3;PL	suggest	Findings of Lapita pottery suggest that early-Polynesians were on these islands close to 3000 years ago.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	These days there are around 20000 people living in the Group.
surround	V;PRS;V.PTCP	surrounding	Neiafu and surrounding villages are home to about a third of the Group’s overall population of around 20000, with the majority of the population living in small villages on the other islands.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	Neiafu is the official port of entry for yachts coming to Vava'u, which attracts over 500 yachts every winter sailing season between June and October.
shelter	V;PRS;SG;3	shelters	A string of islands and reefs along the eastern edge of the Group shelters the area from strong winds and ocean swellS and humpback whales come to these protected waters to give birth.
visit	V;PRS;3;PL	visit	between December and April few people visit and many tour companies and restaurants close down.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	It is on the southern point of the main island of Utu Vava'u and has an attractive setting on one of the world's most beautiful harbours.
offer	V;PRS;SG;3	offers	Neiafu offers all the usual amenities including banks, schools, tour companies, restaurants, cafes and bars, supermarkets, a market and a hospital.
have	V;NFIN	have	It doesn't have a beach but boats to one of the nearby coral atolls with superb sandy beaches are easily available.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Vava'u has a tropical climate with average temperatures up to 29 ºC in January and down to 24º C in June.
occur	V;NFIN	occur	Between November and April, it is more humid and thunderstorms and cyclones do occur.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	From May to September there are southeast trade winds but during the summer months the winds are from the northeast.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	This is the largest island, where Neiafu is found.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	The island provides a home to eleven indigenous bird species and contains Mt. Talau national park, with some of the limited remaining native vegetation.
include	V;PRS;SG;3	includes	Tropical vegetation includes the pandanus or screw pine, the casuarina, and the mulberry tree, the bark of which is used to make tapa cloth.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	It can be seen across the water from Neiafu and is connected by land bridge at the most southern point of ‘Utu Vava’u.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	There are some good beaches and plenty of secluded cove beaches and protected bays with good snorkelling.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	This is some 35 minutes from Neiafu Harbour and provides the main concentration of humpback whales.
lie	V;PRS;SG;3	lies	The island lies in deep water and has sheltered waters and bays which provide a resting place for humpbacks with calves.
grow	V;NFIN	Grow	How to Grow Beavertail Cactus
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	The Beavertail Cactus (Opunitia basilares), found primarily in the Mojave Desert and northwest Mexico; can grow to be about 24 inches (60 cm) high; has pads that look like beavertails; and yields red or purple flowers from spring to early summer.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Growing	Growing Beavertail can be done at any time of year by scattering seeds in a shady bed or, for faster results, planting cuttings in a mix of soil and sand and ensuring that they have a bit of water and plenty of sun.
read	V;IMP;2	Read	Read the following steps to find out how to grow Beavertail cactus.
purchase	V;IMP;2	Purchase	Purchase Beavertail cactus seeds.
buy	V;PST;V.PTCP	bought	These can be bought at garden centers or purchased online.
scatter	V;IMP;2	Scatter	Scatter the seeds.
scatter	V;IMP;2	scatter	In a shady garden bed, simply scatter the seeds on the soil.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's no need to cover them, unless you want to use netting to keep the birds off.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep the soil moist until the seeds germinate.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure that the garden bed is in the shade.
need	V;PRS;3;PL	need	Beavertail cacti seeds need shade to grow, whereas cuttings need plenty of direct sunlight.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	growing	Be aware that growing Beavertail cactus from seeds takes much longer than growing one from a cutting.
take	V;NFIN	take	It can take as long as 4 to 4 years before you get flowers.
obtain	V;IMP;2	Obtain	Obtain Beavertail cuttings.
purchase	V;NFIN	purchase	You can purchase them online or at a local garden center.
own	V;PRS;2;SG	own	If you already own a Beavertail cactus, you can make your own cuttings.
do	V;NFIN	do	To do this, choose pads (the flat, oval-shaped sections that make up a cactus) that are 6 months old or older and cut them at the base.
form	V;NFIN	form	In order to form a callous and prevent rot, cure the cuttings by sitting the pad upright in a dry, dark place for 10 to 14 days.
plant	V;IMP;2	Plant	Plant the cuttings.
prepare	V;IMP;2	Prepare	Prepare a potting mix that is equal parts soil and rough pumice or sand and fill the appropriate number of pots with it.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure there are holes in the center of the base of each pot to allow for good drainage.
set	V;IMP;2	Set	Set the cuttings upright in the potting mix about an inch (2.5 cm.) deep.
plant	V;PRS;2;SG	plant	If you plant the cuttings too deep, they might rot.
put	V;IMP;2	Put	Put some rocks around the pad to anchor it in place.
choose	V;IMP;2	Choose	Choose a spot for your Beavertail cactus cuttings.
live	V;PRS;2;SG	live	If you live in an area with strong summer sun, position the cuttings so that the broad sides of the cuttings face east and west while the slim sides face north and south.
prevent	V;NFIN	prevent	This will prevent the cuttings from burning during the hottest part of the day.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure the spot is sheltered from any cold winter winds.
wait	V;IMP;2	Wait	Wait for a month.
water	V;IMP;2	water	Do not water the cutting for the first month.
provide	V;NFIN	provide	The moisture in the pad will provide enough nutrients for roots to grow.
cause	V;NFIN	cause	Too much water might cause rot.
sprout	V;PST;V.PTCP	sprouted	After 1 month, roots will have sprouted and you can water the cutting for the first time.
let	V;IMP;2	let	Always let the soil dry before you water the cuttings.
root	V;PST;V.PTCP	rooted	Once fully rooted, they are extremely drought resistant.
fertilize	V;IMP;2	Fertilize	Fertilize the plants.
grow	V;PRS;SG;3	grows	During the spring, summer, and fall, the Beavertail cactus grows and will benefit from a balanced fertilizer.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let the cuttings grow.
form	V;NFIN	form	From the first pad, many more will form.
bear	V;NFIN	bear	The second or third pad will bear some flowers, but wait before harvesting them or you might affect the size of future harvests.
get	V;NFIN	Get	How to Get Rid of Mice Naturally
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Having	Having mice in your home is both a nuisance and a health hazard.
cause	V;NFIN	cause	They can cause property damage, create a mess, and produce unpleasant smells.
repel	V;PRS;V.PTCP	repelling	In addition, repelling and removing rodents from your home can be a hassle if you don't know what you are doing.
use	V;PRS;2;SG	use	It can also be dangerous if you use toxic chemicals and poisons.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	It's a good thing, then, that there are some simple ways to effectively get mice out of your home without the use of toxic chemicals and poisons.
discourage	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Discouraging	Method 1 Discouraging Mice Naturally
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep your home clean.
draw	V;PST;V.PTCP	drawn	Many times mice are drawn into homes on the search for food.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	If there is nothing to eat, mice will be less likely to stay.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep all food stored properly in airtight containers or in places that are safe from mouse attack. [1]
apply	V;IMP;2	Apply	Apply peppermint oil.
try	V;NFIN	try	The smell is simply too intense for rodents and they will not try to go near it.
help	V;PRS;SG;3	helps	It also helps to mask the scent of any tasty morsels that have been missed when cleaning.
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	It also gives the house a lovely smell, and is not toxic, unlike many synthetic chemical treatments.
get	V;NFIN	get	You can get peppermint oil in most health food stores and even some major grocery stores. [2]
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place a drop or two on a cotton ball.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place a cotton ball under or near your garbage can to deter mice from getting into it.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place the cotton balls in areas where mice are likely to enter your house, by doorways, or heat vents, etc.
need	V;PRS;3;PL	need	Peppermint oil cotton balls need to be replaced after 5 - 7 days, depending upon the quantity of oil you put on it.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try growing peppermint plants near the entryways.
use	V;NFIN	use	You can use the mint in cooking, as well as it serving a deterrent purpose.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use dried snake feces.
visit	V;IMP;2	Visit	Visit a local reptile center, zoo, or pet store and ask for some dried snake poo.
find	V;NFIN	find	You can also sometimes find someone who owns a pet snake.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place near entrances and in places where mice seem to go.
keep	V;NFIN	keep	This will keep mice away. [3]
keep	V;NFIN	keep	Be sure to keep the feces out of the reach of children and pets.
zap	V;IMP;2	Zap	Zap mice with ultrasonic sounds.
be	V;PRS;3;PL	are	There are electronic units sold that emit an ultrasonic beeping sound that rodents hate.
work	V;PRS;3;PL	work	These work only on a single line of sight, so you need to know the exact path the mice are taking.
work	V;NFIN	work	The units may only work for a short time because mice can become used to them. [4]
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use a commercial, organic deterrent.
specialize	V;PRS;3;PL	specialize	Many companies that specialize in organic garden and home products make mice repellents that are better for the environment than traditional poisons.
use	V;PRS;3;PL	use	Many of these products use natural ingredients, such as peppermint, that are known to be disagreeable to mice.
kill	V;NFIN	kill	They will not kill mice but they should keep them out of areas where the product is applied.
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	If you want a specific product, try doing a online search to see where it is available locally.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get a cat.
take	V;NFIN	take	A house cat can take care of the problem.
spend	V;PRS;3;PL	spend	Cats that spend time outdoors are more likely to hunt to find their own food and may track down that mouse.
have	V;PRS;3;PL	have	Just be aware, not all cats have enough energy, interest or training to hunt mice.
have	V;PRS;3;PL	have	Kittens typically have to be taught by the mother to easily become a mouser.
feed	V;PST;V.PTCP	food	Well-food indoor kitties might just see the mouse as a toy, and grow quickly bored of it after scaring it half witless.
know	V;NFIN	know	Shelters and adoption agencies often do not know if a cat is a good mouse hunter, and generally do not test for this.
socialize	V;PST;V.PTCP	socialized	"Barn cats" may not be socialized well with humans, although used to hunting mice for food.
have	V;NFIN	have	Even if you do not have a cat, you can use used kitty litter to deter mice.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place tubs of used kitty litter around entrances of the house.
sniff	V;NFIN	sniff	The mice will sniff the smell of cat urine and clear off in a jiffy.
have	V;PRS;2;SG	have	If you have a large infestation, a cat may not be enough to totally get rid of it.
eliminate	V;PRS;2;SG	eliminate	However, once you eliminate the infestation, a cat will probably prevent reinfestation. [5]
put	V;IMP;2	Put	Put your trash cans far from your home.
smell	V;PRS;3;PL	smell	Mice smell garbage and get attracted to your house.
keep	V;PRS;2;SG	keep	If you keep the cans away from the house, they are less likely to take up residence in your home.
promote	V;IMP;2	Promote	Promote the nesting of birds of prey in your yard.
build	V;IMP;2	Build	Build a shelter box in your garden to attract some onto your property.
take	V;NFIN	take	This may take some time, but it's worth the effort if you can attract a wild mouse eater to your yard. [6]
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure the shelter box has no nails or safety hazards.
eat	V;NFIN	eat	A family of barn owls can eat many mice in a night!
attract	V;NFIN	attract	However, you might attract other types of owls or birds of prey with your nesting box.
